1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Progenitor of Abbasid Caliphate (Dynasty)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wilaadat (Birth):
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death):
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbasid
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Mansur
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 3: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaysanites_Shia
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Husayn, there were three different lines of Alidsd which compteted for allegiance of the legitimist faction: Those descended from Hasan and Husayn, the two sons of Ali by his wife Fatima, the daughter of the Pro
2 CONC phet who left no son and the House of Muhammad, the son of Ali by another wife known as the Hanifite.... The first Alid faction to become prominent was the partisans of Muhammad, the son of the Hanifite, who were formed into a soc
2 CONC iety by Kaysan, a freedman of Ali, for the purpose of avenging Hasan and Husayn, Ali's sons by Fatima.  At Muhammad's death (AH 81) https://www.geni.com/people/Muhammad-Hanafiya-%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF-%D8%AD%D9%86%D9%81%DB%8C%D9
2 CONC %87-de-la-tribu-des-Banu-Hanifa-and-imamate-of-Kaysanites-Shia-ancetre-des-Donboli/6000000041516148625?through=6000000041516412481 , the Kaysanites recognized his son Abu Hashim as the 5th Imam until AH 98 when Abu Hashim died chi
2 CONC ldless after bequeathing his claims to Muhammad b. Ali b. Abdullah (d.126) who was not of the House of Ali at all and who became the founder of the Abbasid dynasty which obtained the Khalifate in 132...] ref:p. 9 of A Short Histor
2 CONC y of The Fatimid Khalifate by Lacy O'Leary, lecturer in Aramaic and Syriac, Bristol University.
2 CONT
2 CONT ========
2 CONT
2 CONT After Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya died, his son Abu Hashim claimed the imamate. After his death the Abbasids claimed that on his deathbed Abu Hashim nominated his distant cousin Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Abbas ibn Abdu'l-Mut
2 CONC talib ibn Hashim as the imam. This man's son Abu'l-Abbas Abdullah as-Saffah became the first Abbasid caliph, repudiating Shi'ism, which effectively extinguished the sect that had recognized Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah as an imam.[9
2 CONC ]
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Muhammad_ibn_al-Hanafiyyah
2 CONT
2 CONT =======
2 CONT Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abdallah or Muhammad "al-Imâm" was the son of "Ali ibn Abdallah ibn al-‘Abbas" and grandson of al-‘Abbas ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib, the uncle of the Islamic prophet, Muhammad. He was born in Humaima in Palestine, a
2 CONC nd was the founder of the Abbasid dynasty in Arabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolt of Mukhtar al-Thaqafi When al-Mukhtar announced the revenge of imam al-Husayn, he showed himself as the representative of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, the Promised Mehdi according to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sixth Imam of Kaysanites
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, the imamate of Kaysanites Shia transferred to his son Abu Hashim, who transferred it to Muhammad (present profile), paving the way for the Abbasid dawa and the Abbasid Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Bani Abbas Era (711-1235) ... Mokhtar's followers, called Keysanieh, after Abu Hashem's death https://www.geni.com/people/Abu-Hashim-ben-Mohammad-Hanifa-%D8%A7%D8%A8%D9%88%D9%87%D8%A7%D8%B4%D9%85-%D8%B9%D8%A8%D8%AF%D8%A7%D9%84%D9
2 CONC %87-%D8%A8%D9%86-%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%85%D8%AF-%D8%AD%D9%86%D9%81%DB%8C%D9%87/6000000041516412481?through=6000000041516148625  had sworn allegiance to Mohammad ibn Ali ibn Abbas ibn Abdolmottaleb, calling him Emam and naming themselves
2 CONC  "Ale Abbas Shi'ites....] ref The Book of Iran - Iranian History at a glance, Dr Mahmoud Farrokhpey.
