1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Al-Muqtadi''' (1056 – February 1094) (Arabic: المقتدى ‎) was the Abbasid Caliph in Baghdad from 1075 to 1094.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born to Muhammad, the son of Caliph Al-Qa'im, and Urjuman, an Armenian slave girl.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He was honored by the Seljuk Sultan Malik Shah, during whose reign the Caliphate was recognized throughout the extending range of Seljuk conquest. Arabia, with the Holy Cities now recovered from the Fatimids, acknowledged again th
2 CONC e spiritual jurisdiction of the Abbasids. The Sultan arranged a marriage between his daughter and Al-Muqtadi, possibly planning on the birth of a son who could serve as both Caliph and Sultan. Though the couple had a son, the moth
2 CONC er left with her infant to the court of Isfahan. Following the failure of the marriage, the Sultan grew critical of the Caliph's interference in affairs of state, and sent an order for him to retire to Basra. The death of Malik Sh
2 CONC ah shortly after, however, made the command inoperative.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Bennison, Amira K. The Great Caliphs: The Golden Age of the 'Abbasid Empire. Princeton: Yale University Press, 2009, p. 47.
