2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu Mansur Fulad Sutun''' (Persian: ابو منصور فولاد ستون‎, died 1062) was the last Buyid amir of Fars, ruling more or less continuously from 1048 until his death. He was the son of Abu Kalijar.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Reign
2 CONT * 1.1 Struggle for power
2 CONT * 1.2 Seljuq suzerainty
2 CONT * 2 Notes
2 CONT * 3 References
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Struggle for power==
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Abu Kalijar in 1048, his eldest son Abu Nasr Khusrau Firuz had succeeded him as senior amir in Baghdad and assumed the title "al-Malik al-Rahim." Abu Mansur, however, rose up against his brother and control of F
2 CONC ars. Al-Malik al-Rahim along with his brother Abu Sa'd Khusrau Shah invaded Fars and occupied Shiraz, also managing capturing Abu Mansur during the invasion. However, turmoil between the Turks and Daylamites in his army forced the
2 CONC m to abandon the province and leave it in the hands of Abu Mansur.[1] Abu Mansur then managed to become the ruler of Fars once again, and captured parts of Ahvaz from Al-Malik al-Rahim. Al-Malik al-Rahim, however, managed to recap
2 CONC ture Ahvaz and seize another town named Askar Mukram from Abu Mansur.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1051 or 1052 al-Malik al-Rahim's army again invaded Fars and this time defeated Abu Mansur and his allies. Fars was for the next year or so reunited with Iraq and governed by Abu Sa'd Khusrau Shah on al-Malik al-Rahim's behalf.
2 CONC  Abu Mansur, however, requested the support of the Seljuk sultan Toghrul Beg, and was able to recapture Shiraz in 1053 or 1054. He then recognized Toghrul Beg as his overlord and placed the sultan's name first in the khutba, in fr
2 CONC ont of al-Malik al-Rahim's.[2] In 1055, a Dailamite military leader named Fuladh, captured Shiraz and repelled Abu Mansur from Fars. Fuladh then made an agreement with Al-Malik al-Rahim where he agreed to acknowledge his authority
2 CONC . However, Al-Malik al-Rahim and Abu Sa'd Khusrau Shah, did not trust him, and along with Abu Mansur, reconquered Shiraz from Fuladh. Abu Mansur then agreed to acknowledge the authority of Al-Malik al-Rahim.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Seljuq suzerainty==
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1055 al-Malik al-Rahim was arrested and deposed by Toghrul Beg's forces in Baghdad, bringing an end to Buyid rule in Iraq. Abu Mansur, who once again acknowledged his authority, however, was able to remain in power in 
2 CONC Fars for another seven years as a Seljuk vassal. Constant fighting with his brothers, however, weakened his rule, and in 1062 he was killed while fighting against the Kurdish Shabankara tribal chief Fadluya. The Seljuks shortly af
2 CONC terward entered Shiraz and took control of Fars.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT
