2 CONT Altun Jan Khatun is also mentioned on page 259 in [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVd
2 CONC W8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT The sultan's haram was often large and included his wives and concubines and their attendants and female slaves. His chief wives had their own diwans, persona iqtas and landed estates, and were sometimes able to transmit these by 
2 CONC inheritance. Some disposed of considerable funds as witnessed by their charitable benefactions. Toghril Beg's chief wife Altun-Jan held estates in Iraq. She died in 452/1060 and bequeathed her estates and pensions to the caliph's 
2 CONC daughter. Toghril assigned these to the latter when the marriage contract between him and the caliph's daughter was finally concluded in 454/1062...
