1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samsam_al-Dawla '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu Kalijar Marzuban''', also known as '''Samsam al-Dawla''' (c. 963 – December 998) was the Buyid amir of Iraq (983–987), as well as Fars and Kerman (988 or 989 – 998). He was the second son of 'Adud al-Dawla. The Abbasids rec
2 CONC ognized his succession and conferred upon him the title Samsam al-Dawla. He lacked the qualities of his father 'Adud al-Dawla and failed to have a grip upon his state affairs. His rule was marked by revolts and civil wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Biography
2 CONT * 1.1 Early life and rise
2 CONT * 1.2 Early reign
2 CONT * 1.3 Regaining his lost position
2 CONT * 1.4 Losing power
2 CONT * 2 References
2 CONT * 3 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Biography=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early life and rise==
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Kalijar Marzuban was born in 963, he was the son of Adud al-Dawla and Sayyida ibn Siyahgil, a daughter of Siyahgil,[1] an Gilite ruler.[2] Thus making Abu Kalijar Marzuban distantly related to Ziyarid dynasty, who were descend
2 CONC ed from a sister of the Gilite ruler Harusindan, who was the father of Siyahgil.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT During 'Adud al-Dawla's lifetime, Abu Kalijar Marzuban was assigned the governorships of Buyid Oman and Khuzestan. Despite Marzuban's status as second son (Shirdil being the eldest), he was considered to be his father's heir. This
2 CONC  issue was never completely clarified by 'Adud al-Dawla before his death, resulting in a succession crisis. Marzuban, who was in Baghdad when his father died, at first kept his death secret in order to ensure his succession. When 
2 CONC he made the death of his father public, he took the title "Samsam al-Dawla".
2 CONT
2 CONT Shirdil also laid his claims to the succession, and from his province of Kerman invaded and captured Fars. He took the title "Sharaf al-Dawla". Sharaf al-Dawla's invasion of Fars provided two more of Samsam al-Dawla's brothers, Ta
2 CONC j al-Dawla and Diya' al-Dawla, to set up their own rule in Basra and Khuzestan. In Diyar Bakr, a Kurd named Badh ibn Hasanwaih took power and forced Samsam al-Dawla to confirm him as its ruler. To the north, Samsam al-Dawla's uncl
2 CONC e Fakhr al-Dawla ruled an extensive territory from Ray. The rulers of Basra and Khuzestan soon acknowledged Fakhr al-Dawla as senior amir, making the latter the most powerful of the Buyids and moving the senior amirate from Iraq t
2 CONC o Jibal.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Early reign==
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite Fakhr al-Dawla's power, it was Sharaf al-Dawla who posed the largest threat to Samsam al-Dawla. He recovered Buyid Oman, which had earlier seceded to Samsam al-Dawla. In 983, the Turkic soldiers of Iraq betrayed Samsam al-
2 CONC Dawla, and went towards to the court of Sharaf al-Dawla. However, his relative from his mother's side Ziyar ibn Shahrakawayh managed to make most of them change their mind and stay loyal to Samsam al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 985, a Dailamite chief Saffar ibn Quddawiyah revolted against the authority of Samsam he joined with (Shirdil. Saffar lead a force against Samsam to Baghdad. Samsam sent a stronger force in retaliation consequently Saffar was d
2 CONC efeated. In early 986 Samsam captured Basra and Khuzestan, forcing the two brothers to flee to Fakhr al-Dawla's territory. During the same period, another Dailamite named Asfar ibn Kurdawayh rebelled against Samsam al-Dawla, and c
2 CONC hanged his allegiance to Sharaf al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Asfar quickly changed his mind, and declared allegiance to the latter's other brother Abu Nasr Firuz Kharshadh, who was shortly given the honorific epithet of "Baha' al-Dawla." However, Samsam al-Dawla, with the aid of Fu
2 CONC ladh ibn Manadhar, suppressed the rebellion, imprisoned Baha al-Dawla, and executed his supporters executed, including Bahram ibn Ardashir al-Majusi. Samsam al-Dawla then made peace with Sharaf al-Dawla, and agreed to release Baha
2 CONC  al-Dawla.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Sharaf betrayed Samsam, and quickly marched against him. Sharaf occupied Ahwaz, then sent his forces to Wasit which fell to him in 986 AD. From there Samsam marched to Baghdad. Before any confrontation could take place, t
2 CONC here was a revolt in the army of Samsam, He was therefore defeated and forced to surrender. There upon Baghdad fell to Sharaf and Samsam was put in prison.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Regaining his lost position==
2 CONT
2 CONT Sharaf al-Dawla's death in 988 or 989 provided Samsam al-Dawla with the opportunity to make a return to power. Despite having been partially blinded shortly before Sharaf al-Dawla's death, he managed to escape from prison and with
2 CONC  the aid of Sharaf al-Dawla's former vizier Ala ibn Hasan,[5] wrested control of Fars, Kerman and Khuzestan from his brother Baha' al-Dawla, who had succeeded Sharaf al-Dawla. Both Baha' al-Dawla and his brother found their positi
2 CONC ons threatened by Fakhr al-Dawla. The latter invaded Khuzestan in an attempt to split the two brothers' territories. This act prompted the both of them to draw up an alliance. Samsam al-Dawla recognized Baha' al-Dawla as the ruler
2 CONC  of Iraq and Khuzestan, while he himself kept Arrajan, Fars and Kerman. Both promised to consider each other as equals, and took the title of "king".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 991 Baha' al-Dawla attempted to get rid of Samsam al-Dawla. He took the title of Shâhanshâh and invaded the latter's territory. His forces were defeated, however, and Samsam al-Dawla regained Khuzestan. He even gained control o
2 CONC f the Buyid territories in Oman. In order to further strengthen his position, Samsam al-Dawla decided to recognise Fakhr al-Dawla as senior amir, submitting to his authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Losing power==
2 CONT
2 CONT Fakhr al-Dawla's death in 997, coupled with Samsam al-Dawla's increasing troubles within his realm, made Baha' al-Dawla the strongest of the Buyid princes. He gained the support of the Kurdish ruler Badr ibn Hasanwaih and prepared
2 CONC  for the expedition. The invasion began in December of 998. Scarcely had the campaign begun, however, when Samsam al-Dawla was murdered by one of the sons of 'Izz al-Dawla near Isfahan while fleeing from Shiraz. Baha' al-Dawla too
2 CONC k Shiraz, defeated 'Izz al-Dawla's sons, and reunited Iraq, Fars and Kerman.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Donohue 2003, p. 87.
2 CONT # Madelung 1975, p. 219.
2 CONT # Madelung 1975, p. 211.
2 CONT # The Buwayhid dynasty of Baghdad by M.Kabir
2 CONT # Kennedy 2004, p. 235.
2 CONT
