1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=lQjkJOUcVIwC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Zubeida+Khatun,+wife+of+Malik-Shah&source=bl&ots=5h6pZ2rimG&sig=reGhqSBaBsqKwuYCVdSrdzoi_6I&hl=en&sa=X&ei=vWwyVdW8EMnksAT_mIDwBQ&ved=0CDEQ6
2 CONC AEwAw#v=onepage&q=Aka&f=false '''Continuity and Change in Medieval Persia'''] by Ann K.S. Lambton:
2 CONT
2 CONT Garchasp apparently had another daughter Ata Khatun, who was married to Mahmud b. Muhammad. They had a son Ala al-Daula Ata Khan, who came back to Yazd with his mother Ata Khatun after the death of Mahmud. Ata Khan built a splendi
2 CONC d madrasa in Yazd, which was still in existence when Ahmad b. Husain was writing. When Ata Khan and Ata Khatun came to Yzad, they brought with them Terken Khatun, the daughter of Mahmud b. Muhammad, whose mother was a concubine. A
2 CONC fter the death of Ata Khatun, one of Qavurt’s grandsons asked the hand of Terken Khatun for his son Sulaiman-Shah.
