1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sam_ibn_Wardanruz '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sam ibn Wardanruz''' was the first ruler of the Atabegs of Yazd dynasty from 1141 to 1188.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam was appointed as atabeg by Ahmad Sanjar in 1141, where he married one of the daughters of the Kakuyid Garshasp II. He is described as a weak ruler, and was in 1188 replaced by his more capable brother Langar ibn Wardanruz. Sam
2 CONC  later died in 1193.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * C. Edmund, Bosworth (2007). Historic Cities of the Islamic World. Retrieved 26 January 2014.
2 CONT * S. C., Fairbanks (2001). "ATĀBAKĀN-E YAZD". Encyclopaedia Iranica, Online Edition. Retrieved 26 January 2014.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atabegs_of_Yazd '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT The '''Atabegs of Yazd''' (Persian: اتابکان یزد‎‎, Atābakān-e Yazd) were a local dynasty, which ruled the city of Yazd from about 1141 to 1319. They succeeded the Kakuyids to whom they were linked by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the names of the earlier members of the dynasty, it seems they were ethnically Persian, but like the Hazaraspids they had adopted the Turkish title of Atabeg.[1] Most of the Atabegs of Yazd were tributaries to the Saljuqs and
2 CONC  the Mongol Il-Khans until they were finally overthrown by the Muzaffarids.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT =List of rulers=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sam ibn Wardanruz (1141 - 1188)'''
2 CONT * Langar ibn Wardanruz (1188 - 1207)
2 CONT * Wardanruz ibn Langar (1207 - 1219)
2 CONT * Isfahsalar ibn Langar (1219 - 1229)
2 CONT * Mahmud Shah ibn Isfahsalar (1229 - 1241)
2 CONT * Salghur Shah ibn Mahmud Shah (1241 - 1252)
2 CONT * Toghan Shah ibn Salghur Shah (1252 - 1272)
2 CONT * Ala al-Dawla ibn Toghan Shah (1272 - 1275)
2 CONT * Yusuf Shah ibn Toghan Shah (1275 - 1297)
2 CONT * Mongol occupation (1297-1315)
2 CONT * Hajji Shah ibn Yusuf Shah (1315-1319)
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Clifford Edmund Bosworth, The New Islamic Dynasties: A Chronological and Genealogical Manual, Columbia University, 1996. Excerpt 1 pp 209: "The Atabegs of Yazd" (1141-1297)" Excerpt 2: "From the names of the earlier members at l
2 CONC east, it seems they were ethnically Persian, but, like the Hazaraspids, they adopted the Turkish title of Atabeg"
2 CONT # S. C. Fairbanks, "ATĀBAKĀN-E YAZD" in Encyclopaedia Iranica. [1] (accessed October 2010)
2 CONT
