1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barkiyaruq '''Wikipedia''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Abu al-Muzaffar Rukn ud-Din Barkyaruq ibn Malikshah''' (Persian: ابو المظفر رکن الدین رکن الدین برکیارق بن ملکشاه‎), better known as Barkyaruq, a Turkic word which means (firm, unwavering light),[1] was the sultan of the Great 
2 CONC Seljuq empire from 1092 to 1105.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Contents=
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Early life
2 CONT * 2 Reign
2 CONT * 3 Affairs of the Empire during his reign
2 CONT * 4 References
2 CONT * 5 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT =Early life=
2 CONT
2 CONT Barkiyaruq was born in 1079/1080,[2] he was the oldest son of Malik Shah I,[3] and a Seljuq princess. He had three brothers named Mahmud I, Ahmed Sanjar, Mehmed I, Dawud, and Ahmad.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his youth, the succession to the Seljuq sultanate was complicated by the death of two of his half-brothers: Dawud (died 1082) and Ahmad (died 1088), whom both were sons of the Kara-Khanid Princess Turkan Khatun, she also ha
2 CONC d a named Mahmud (born 1087) which she wanted to succeed his father, while the vizier Nizam al-Mulk and most of the Seljuq army was in favor of Barkiyaruq,[4] the oldest of all Malik Shah's living sons and born to a Seljuq princes
2 CONC s. Turkan Khatun then allied with Taj al-Mulk Abu'l Ghana'im to try to remove Nizam from his post. Nizam was later assassinated in 1092, which made Barkiyaruq lose a powerful supporter. Barkiyaruq's father eventually died some mon
2 CONC ths later. Turkhan Khatun then took the opportunity of his death, and with the support of Taj al-Mulk, put her 4 year old Mahmud I on the throne, while Barkiyaruq was proclaimed as Sultan of the Seljuq Empire at Ray by the faction
2 CONC  of the dead vizier Nizam al-Mulk.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Mahmud I was not the only Seljuq claimant to the throne, several other Seljuq princes such as Arslan-Argun, Mehmed I, and Tutush I, also claimed the throne.[5] Taj al-Mulk was later assassinated by the ghulams of Nizam al
2 CONC -Mulk,[6] while Turkhan Khatun and her son Mahmud I died in 1094. One year later, Barkiyaruq clashed with Tutush I at Ray, where Barkiyaruq managed to emerge victorious and kill Tutush I along with his supporter Ali ibn Faramurz.[
2 CONC 7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1105, Barkiyaruq died in Borujerd, and was succeeded by his son Malik Shah II. It has been reported that his body was returned to Isfahan. However, some people say[who?] his tomb is in 5 km north of Borujerd, where today is a h
2 CONC istorical monument called Zavvarian.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Affairs of the Empire during his reign=
2 CONT
2 CONT During Barkiyaruq's short reign, he had five viziers, three of them were the children of Nizam al-Mulk; Izz al-Mulk Husain, Mu'ayyid al-Mulk and Fakhr al-Mulk.[8] The two other viziers were Abd-al-Dihistani Jalil and Khatir al-Mul
2 CONC k Abu Mansur Maybudi. During his reign, Barkiyaruq's mostly focused on a way to find money to keep the expenses of the state.
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barkiyaruq#References '''1-8''']
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bosworth, C. E. (1968). "The Political and Dynastic History of the Iranian World (A.D. 1000–1217)". In Frye, R. N. The Cambridge History of Iran, Volume 5: The Saljuq and Mongol periods. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. pp
2 CONC . 1–202. ISBN 0-521-06936-X.
