1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''From Banu 'Adi Clan of Quraysh Tribe.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Second Caliph of Islam''' Succeeded by https://www.geni.com/people/Caliph-%CA%BBUthm%C4%81n-bin-%CA%BBAff%C4%81n/6000000003153535504
2 CONT
2 CONT When attained majority,father,Khattab set me to the task of grazing camels,an occupation which was then the favorite national calling in Arabia. Acquired skill in tracing genealogy from his father-attained perfection in wrestling-
2 CONC during youth,literally jumped on to my horse's back-prior to my conversion to Islam the Quraish had invested me with the ambassadorial office and my brain was a repository of the choicest verses of the famous poets-At the time of 
2 CONC the advent of the Prophet, I was amongst only 17 persons in the whole clan of Quraish who could read and write-to earn livelihood by  trading travelled to Persia, & Syria,paid visits to several  Arabian & Persian princes-was 27 ye
2 CONC ars old when Prophet declared Islam.After the death of Amir-al-Momineen,Allah bestowed this exalted office laden with heavy responsibilty to be the Amir -ul-Momineen, or Leader of all Muslims.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was born in Mecca to the Banu Adi clan, which was responsible for arbitration among the tribes.[1] 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Father: al-Khattab ibn Nufayl 
2 CONT *Mother: Hantama bint Hisham, from the tribe of Banu Makhzum. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar ibn al-Khattab, the second of the Rashidun Caliphs was the son of Khattab ibn Nufayl ibn 'Abdul 'Uzza ibn Riyah ibn Abdullah ibn Qurut ibn Razah ibn 'Adiyy ibn Ka’ab ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib al-Quraishi al-Adawai. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His lineage converges with that of Muhammad in Ka'b ibn Lu'ayy ibn Ghalib.
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Family tree of Umar
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_tree_of_Umar
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar married nine women in his lifetime and had fourteen children: ten sons and four daughters.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Wives
2 CONT
2 CONT The known wives of Umar are:
2 CONT # Zaynab bint Maz'un, she was the mother of Hafsa, Abd Allah and Abd al-Rahman al-Akbar.
2 CONT # Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal, she was divorced by Umar. She was the mother of Ubayd Allah and Zayd al-Asghar.
2 CONT # Qurayba bint Abi Umayya, divorced by Umar in 628.
2 CONT # Jamila bint Thabit, She married Umar about between May 627 and May 628.[154] They had one son, Asim.[155][156][157][154][158]
2 CONT # Atiqa bint Zayd, she was married to Umar and had a son named Iyad.
2 CONT # Umm Hakim bint al-Harith ibn Hisham, She was married to Umar[159] in 634 and was mother of Fatima.
2 CONT # Umm Kulthum bint Ali[160][161] from this marriage Umar had a son named Zayd and a daughter named Ruqayya. This is, however, the Sunni view. The Shi'a do not accept that such a marriage took place. In fact, few Sunnis scholars ma
2 CONC intain that Umar's wife Umm Kulthum was actually Abu Bakr's daughter who was raised in Ali's house.[162]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sons
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Umar are:[citation needed]
2 CONT # Abd Allah, son of Zaynab bint Maz'un.
2 CONT # Abd al-Rahman, son of Zaynab bint Maz'un.
2 CONT # Zayd, son of Umm Kulthum bint Ali.[163]
2 CONT # Ubayd Allah, son of Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal.
2 CONT # Zayd, son of Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal.
2 CONT # Asim, son of Jamila bint Thabit.
2 CONT # Iyad, son of Atiqa bint Zayd.
2 CONT # Abd al-Rahman Abu'l-Mujabbar
2 CONT # Abd al-Rahman "Abu Shahmah" ibn Umar
2 CONT # Abd Allah
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughters of Umar are:[citation needed]
2 CONT # Hafsa, daughter of Zaynab bint Maz'un.
2 CONT # Fatima, daughter of Umm Hakim bint al-Harith ibn Hisham.
2 CONT # Ruqayya, daughter of Umm Kulthum bint Ali.
2 CONT # Zaynab
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umar
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umar_ibn_al-Chattab
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 3: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banu_Adi
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Umar ibn al-Khattab' (592-644)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Second of the four "rightly guided" caliphs. Umar was born in Meccaaround 581 to the Adi clan of the Quraish tribe. Umar belonged to afamily of average class, but he was able to become literate, and waswell known for his physical 
2 CONC strength, becoming a champion wrestler.When Muhammad first declared his message of Islam, Umar believed Islamwas heretical rhetoric against the Quraish and his ancestors, and heresolved to kill Muhammad. He was stopped on his way 
2 CONC to Muhammad'shouse, however, with news of his sister's conversion to Islam. Umarwas initially angered by the news, but after reading some of the Quranhe was instantly changed. Rather than killing Muhammad, he determinedto accept I
2 CONC slam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was part of the first emigration to Medina, and became animportant companion of Muhammad. He participated in all of the Muslimbattles against the Quraish. Upon the death of Muhammad, Umar was insuch a state of despair that he
2 CONC  threatened to decapitate anyone whosaid that Muhammad was dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Bakr became the first successor to Muhammad. During Abu Bakr'sshort reign as caliph, Umar was an important advisor to him, and AbuBakr selected Umar as his successor prior to his death. Umar reignedas caliph from 633 until his
2 CONC  assassination in 644. Umar's time ascaliph saw the Islamic empire grow at an unprecedented rate, takingIraq and parts of Iran from the Sassanids, and thereby ending thatempire, and taking Egypt, Palestine, Syria, North Africa and
2 CONC  Armeniafrom the Byzantines. Umar also codified Islamic law, and was known forhis simple lifestyle and modest living. A famous story tells of himarriving in Jerusalem walking beside his camel upon which his servantwas sitting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was murdered in 644 by a Persian slave who was angered by apersonal quarrel with Umar; he stabbed the caliph six times as Umarled prayers in Masjid al Nabawi. Umar died two days later, and isburied alongside Muhammad and Abu 
2 CONC Bakr. Prior to dying, he appointed acouncil of six men to elect his successor from amongst themselves,chosing Uthman ibn Affan. Umar is most recognized for originating mostof the major political institutions of the Muslim state an
2 CONC dstabilizing the rapidly expanding Arab empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Umar, also spelled Omar (Arabic: عمر بن الخطاب‎, translit. ʿUmar ibn Al-Khattāb, Umar Son of Al-Khattab, born c.583 CE – died 3 November 644 CE), was one of the most powerful and influential Muslim caliphs in history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuffa during Caliph Umar's era (637–644) The Arabs, led by Caliph ʻUmar ibn Khattāb, conquered Iraq and began ruling Suristan around 637. Umar, who assigned the land of the Jews in Arabia to his warriors, ordered the relocation of
2 CONC  the Jews of Khaybar to a strip of land in Kufa in 640.
2 CONT
2 CONT Caliph Umar was a senior Sahaba of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. He succeeded Abu Bakr (632–634) as the second caliph of the Rashidun Caliphate on 23 August 634. He was an expert Islamic jurist known for his pious and just nature,
2 CONC  which earned him the epithet Al-Farooq ("the one who distinguishes between right and wrong"). He is sometimes referred to as Umar I by historians of Islam, since a later Umayyad caliph, Umar II, also bore that name. According to 
2 CONC Sunnis, Umar is the second greatest of the Sahaba after Abu Bakr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Umar, the caliphate expanded at an unprecedented rate, ruling the Sasanian Empire and more than two-thirds of the Byzantine Empire. His attacks against the Sasanian Empire resulted in the conquest of Persia in fewer than two
2 CONC  years (642–644).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Jewish tradition, Umar set aside the Christian ban on Jews and allowed them into Jerusalem and to worship
2 CONT
2 CONT In 610 Muhammad started preaching the message of Islam. Like many others in Mecca, Umar opposed Islam and he even threatened to kill Muhammad. He resolved to defend the traditional polytheistic religion of Arabia. He was adamant a
2 CONC nd cruel in opposing Muhammad and very prominent in persecuting Muslims. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He recommended Muhammad's death. He firmly believed in the unity of the Quraish and saw the new faith of Islam as a cause of division and discord.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to persecution, Muhammad ordered some of his followers to migrate to Abyssinia. As a small group of Muslims migrated Umar became worried about the future unity of the Quraish and decided to have Muhammad assassinated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar converted to Islam in 616, one year after the Migration to Abyssinia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar started the welfare state which continued under the Ummayad power. [The welfare state of both the Muslim and the non-Muslim poor started by Umar ibn al Khattab had also continued (under the Ummayad)...]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Caliph Umar (634-644 CE) launched an offensive against the Sassanid Persian Empire in 642 and by 651 the Empire was destroyed. But parts of Khorasan where held by Sasanian loyalists helped by their Hephthalite allies.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Banu Najjar were the maternal tribe of the Prophet's grandfather Abdul-Muttalib, they resided in Madinah, and after the Prophet's emigration from Makkah he settled with them in Madinah, and it is at their settlement that the c
2 CONC urrent Mosque of the Prophet stands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not to be confused with Banu Nadir, another Jewish tribe.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Zaidiyyah Shia accept Umar and Abu Bakr as legitimate Rashidun caliphs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar is considered as a political genius, as an architect of the Islamic Empire he is regarded as the 52nd most influential figure in history. Umar remained politically stagnant during Muhammad's era, however after his death, it w
2 CONC as Umar's brilliance that Abu Bakr was elected caliph, despite massive initial confrontations at Saqifah.[citation needed] Umar successfully broke the alliance of the tribes of Medina who claim the caliphate to be their right, pav
2 CONC ing the way for the succession of Abu Bakr. During Abu Bakr's era, he actively participated as his secretary and main adviser. After succeeding Abu Bakr as caliph, Umar won over the hearts of Bedouin tribes by emancipating all the
2 CONC ir prisoners and slaves taken during the Ridda wars, his excellent oratory skills helped him to heighten his popularity graph, mostly among the poor and underprivileged people.[citation needed] He proved himself as an excellent ma
2 CONC nager during the year of the great Famine when his dynamic abilities saved millions from starvation.[citation needed] He is best known for building up an efficient administrative structure of the empire, that held together his vas
2 CONC t realm. He organized an effective network of intelligence, partly a reason for his strong grip on his bureaucracy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His judicial reforms were fairly modern and advanced in nature when compared to contemporary systems of his era.[citation needed] He opposed the construction of the present day Suez Canal, as it posed a threat to the security of M
2 CONC edina. One of the reasons for the compactness of his political rule in the conquered lands is reputed to be his policy of tolerance to their religious beliefs and imposition of far lower taxes on them as compared to the Sassanid P
2 CONC ersian empire and Byzantine Empire.[citation needed] Their local administration was kept untouched and several of the former Byzantine and Persian officials were retained on their services under Umar's governors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar never appointed governors for more than two years, for they might get influence in their county. He dismissed his most successful general Khalid ibn Walid, due to his immense popularity and growing influence that he saw as a 
2 CONC menace to his authority. Rather than tenacious conquest he stressed more on consolidating his rule in the conquered land, a fact that saved the Byzantine empire from complete disappearance. Umar is reported to have wished an offic
2 CONC ial tour across his domain to personally examine the condition of his subjects. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ''From Banu 'Adi Clan of Quraysh Tribe.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khattab_ibn_Nufayl
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hisham_ibn_al-Mughirah
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 3: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umar
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 4: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banu_Adi
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Khattab ibn Nufayl was an Arab from the tribe of Quraish. He lived during the 6th century and was a contemporary of Muhammad. He was also the father of Umar ibn al-Khattab, who would later become Muslim and is regarded by Sunni Mu
2 CONC slims as the second "Rightly guided Caliph" (Arabic "Rashidun"). Thus, he was an ancestor of Sahaba. [The term aṣ-ṣaḥābah (Arabic: الصحابة‎ meaning "the companions", from the verb صَحِبَ meaning "accompany", "keep company with", "
2 CONC associate with") refers to the companions, disciples, scribes and family of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. ]
2 CONT
2 CONT One time, his nephew Said ibn Zayd taunted him for worshiping idols. At that, he seethed with anger and strode up to Zayd, slapped him on the face and shouted:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Damn you! We still hear from you such stupidity. We have borne it until our patience is exhausted." He then incited a number of violent people to harass and persecute his nephew and make life extremely uncomfortable for him. He h
2 CONC ad a son, Umar, who would accompany him in persecuting the Muslims. His other children included a daughter, Fatimah bint al-Khattab,and a son Zayd ibn al-Khattab. The daughter married the hanif Said ibn Zayd, and later would both 
2 CONC become Muslims. However, they hid their new faith from Khattab and Umar due to fear of persecution. [1]. Eventually, Umar discovered their faith, and Umar became a Muslim that very day.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son, Umar, who would accompany him in persecuting the Muslims. His other children included a daughter, Fatimah bint al-Khattab,and a son Zayd ibn al-Khattab. The daughter married the hanif Said ibn Zayd, and later would b
2 CONC oth become Muslims. However, they hid their new faith from Khattab and Umar due to fear of persecution. Eventually, Umar discovered their faith, and Umar became a Muslim that very day.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Khattab ibn Nufayl was an Arab from the tribe of Quraish. He lived during the 6th century and was a contemporary of Muhammad. He was also the father of Umar ibn al-Khattab, who would later become Muslim and is regarded by Sunni Mu
2 CONC slims as the second "Rightly guided Caliph" (Arabic "Rashidun"). Thus, he was an ancestor of Sahaba.
2 CONT
2 CONT One time, his nephew Said ibn Zayd taunted him for worshiping idols. At that, he seethed with anger and strode up to Zayd, slapped him on the face and shouted:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Damn you! We still hear from you such stupidity. We have borne it until our patience is exhausted." He then incited a number of violent people to harass and persecute his nephew and make life extremely uncomfortable for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had a son, Umar, who would accompany him in persecuting the Muslims. His other children included a daughter, Fatimah bint al-Khattab,and a son Zayd ibn al-Khattab. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter married the hanif Said ibn Zayd, and later would both become Muslims. 
2 CONT
2 CONT However, they hid their new faith from Khattab and Umar due to fear of persecution. Eventually, Umar discovered their faith, and Umar became a Muslim that very day.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ''From Banu 'Adi Clan of Quraysh Tribe.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banu_Adi
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umar
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (Arabic: عُمَرْ بِنْ ٱلْخَطَّابْ, romanized: ʿUmar bin al-Khaṭṭāb, also spelled Omar, c. 582/583 – 644) was the second Rashidun caliph, ruling from August 634 until his assassination in 644. He succeeded Abu B
2 CONC akr (r. 632–634) as the second caliph of the Rashidun Caliphate on 23 August 634. Umar was a senior companion and father-in-law of the Islamic prophet Muhammad. He was also an expert Muslim jurist known for his pious and just natu
2 CONC re, which earned him the epithet Al-Fārūq ("the one who distinguishes (between right and wrong)"). The title Amir al-Mu'minin was held by Umar ibn Khattab who was also the first one to be given this title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar initially opposed Muhammad, his distant Qurayshite kinsman and later son-in-law. Following his conversion to Islam in 616, he became the first Muslim to openly pray at the Kaaba. Umar participated in almost all battles and ex
2 CONC peditions under Muhammad, who bestowed the title al-Fārūq upon him, for his judgements. After Muhammad's death in June 632, Umar pledged allegiance to Abu Bakr (r. 632–634) as the first caliph and served as the closest adviser to 
2 CONC the latter until August 634, when the dying Abu Bakr nominated Umar as his successor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Umar, the caliphate expanded at an unprecedented rate, ruling the Sasanian Empire and more than two-thirds of the Byzantine Empire.[3] His attacks against the Sasanian Empire resulted in the conquest of Persia in less than t
2 CONC wo years (642–644). According to Jewish tradition, Umar set aside the Christian ban on Jews and allowed them into Jerusalem and to worship.[4] Umar was assassinated by the Persian slave Abu Lu'lu'a Firuz in 644.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar is generally viewed by historians to be one of the most powerful and influential Muslim caliphs in history.[5] He is revered in the Sunni Islamic tradition as a great just ruler and paragon of Islamic virtues,[6] and some had
2 CONC iths identify him as the second greatest of the Sahabah after Abu Bakr.[7][8] He is viewed negatively in the Twelver Shia tradition.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT Umar was born in Mecca to the Banu Adi clan, which was responsible for arbitration among the tribes.[10] His father was Khattab ibn Nufayl and his mother was Hantama bint Hisham, from the tribe of Banu Makhzum. In his youth he use
2 CONC d to tend to his father's camels in the plains near Mecca. His merchant father was famed for his intelligence among his tribe.[11] Umar himself said: "My father, al-Khattab, was a ruthless man. He used to make me work hard; if I d
2 CONC idn't work he used to beat me and he used to work me to exhaustion."[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite literacy being uncommon in pre-Islamic Arabia, Umar learned to read and write in his youth. Though not a poet himself, he developed a love for poetry and literature.[13] According to the tradition of Quraish, while still i
2 CONC n his teenage years, Umar learned martial arts, horse riding and wrestling. He was tall, physically powerful and a renowned wrestler.[13][14] He was also a gifted orator who succeeded his father as an arbitrator among the tribes.[
2 CONC 15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar became a merchant and made several journeys to Rome and Persia, where he is said to have met various scholars and analyzed Roman and Persian societies. As a merchant he was unsuccessful.[13][16] Like others around him, Umar w
2 CONC as fond of drinking in his pre-Islamic days.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early military career
2 CONT Opposition to Islam
2 CONT In 610, Muhammad started preaching the message of Islam. However, like many others in Mecca, Umar opposed Islam and even threatened to kill Muhammad. He resolved to defend the traditional polytheistic religion of Arabia. He was ad
2 CONC amant and cruel in opposing Muhammad, and very prominent in persecuting Muslims.[18] He recommended Muhammad's death.[19] He firmly believed in the unity of the Quraish and saw the new faith of Islam as a cause of division and dis
2 CONC cord.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to persecution, Muhammad ordered some of his followers to migrate to Abyssinia. When a small group of Muslims migrated, Umar became worried about the future unity of the Quraish and decided to have Muhammad assassinated.[20]
2 CONT
2 CONT Conversion to Islam and service under Muhammad
2 CONT Umar converted to Islam in 616, one year after the Migration to Abyssinia. The story was recounted in Ibn Ishaq's Sīrah. On his way to murder Muhammad, Umar met his best friend Nu'aym ibn Abd Allah who had secretly converted to Is
2 CONC lam but had not told Umar. When Umar informed him that he had set out to kill Muhammad, Nu'aym said, “By God, you have deceived yourself, O Umar! Do you think that Banu Abd al-Manaf would let you run around alive once you had kill
2 CONC ed their son Muhammad? Why don't you return to your own house and at least set it straight?"[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Nuaimal Hakim told him to inquire about his own house where his sister and her husband had converted to Islam. Upon arriving at her house, Umar found his sister and brother-in-law Saeed bin Zaid (Umar's cousin) reciting the verses
2 CONC  of the Quran from sura Ta-Ha.[22] He started quarreling with his brother-in-law. When his sister came to rescue her husband, he also started quarreling with her. Yet still they kept on saying "you may kill us but we will not give
2 CONC  up Islam". Upon hearing these words, Umar slapped his sister so hard that she fell to the ground bleeding from her mouth. When he saw what he did to his sister, he calmed down out of guilt and asked his sister to give him what sh
2 CONC e was reciting. His sister replied in the negative and said "You are unclean, and no unclean person can touch the Scripture." He insisted, but his sister was not prepared to allow him to touch the pages unless he washed his body. 
2 CONC Umar at last gave in. He washed his body and then began to read the verses that were: Verily, I am Allah: there is no God but Me; so serve Me (only), and establish regular prayer for My remembrance (Quran 20:14). He wept and decla
2 CONC red, "Surely this is the word of Allah. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah." On hearing this, Khabbab came out from inside and said: "O, Umar! Glad tidings for you. Yesterday Muhammad prayed to Allah, 'O, Allah
2 CONC ! Strengthen Islam with either Umar or Abu Jahl, whomsoever Thou likest.' It seems that his prayer has been answered in your favour."[23]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar then went to Muhammad with the same sword he intended to kill him with and accepted Islam in front of him and his companions. Umar was 39 years old when he accepted Islam.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to one account, after his conversion to Islam Umar openly prayed at the Kaaba as the Quraish chiefs, Abu Jahl and Abu Sufyan, reportedly watched in anger.[25] This further helped the Muslims to gain confidence in practic
2 CONC ing Islam openly. At this stage Umar even challenged anyone who dared to stop the Muslims from praying, although no one dared to interfere with Umar when he was openly praying.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar's conversion to Islam granted power to the Muslims and to the Islamic faith in Mecca. It was after this event that Muslims offered prayers openly in Masjid al-Haram for the first time. Abdullah ibn Masud said,
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar's embracing Islam was our victory, his migration to Medina was our success, and his reign a blessing from Allah. We didn't offer prayers in al-Haram Mosque until Umar had accepted Islam. When he accepted Islam, the Quraysh we
2 CONC re compelled to let us pray in the Mosque.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Migration to Medina
2 CONT In 622 CE, due to the safety offered by Yathrib (later renamed Medīnat an-Nabī, or simply Medina), Muhammad ordered his followers to migrate to Medina. Most Muslims migrated at night fearing Quraish resistance, but Umar is reporte
2 CONC d to have left openly during the day saying: "Any one who wants to make his wife a widow and his children orphans should come and meet me there behind that cliff."[27][28] Umar migrated to Medina accompanied by his cousin and brot
2 CONC her-in-law Saeed ibn Zaid.[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT Life in Medina
2 CONT vte
2 CONT Campaigns of Umar
2 CONT During Umar's reign as caliph Muhammad ibn Muslamah was assigned the office of Chief Inspector of Accountability. Muslims remained in peace in Medina for approximately a year before the Quraish raised an army to attack them. In 62
2 CONC 4, Umar participated in the first battle between Muslims and Quraish of Mecca i.e., the Battle of Badr. In 625, he took part in the Battle of Uhud. In the second phase of the battle, when Khalid ibn Walid's cavalry attacked the Mu
2 CONC slim rear, turning the tide of battle, rumours of Muhammad's death were spread and many Muslim warriors were routed from the battlefield, Umar among them. However, hearing that Muhammad was still alive, he went to Muhammad at the 
2 CONC mountain of Uhud and prepared for the defence of the hill.[29] Later in the year Umar was a part of a campaign against the Jewish tribe of Banu Nadir. In 625, Umar's daughter Hafsah was married to Muhammad.[30] Later in 627, he pa
2 CONC rticipated in the Battle of the Trench and also in the Battle of Banu Qurayza.[31] In 628, Umar witnessed the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah.[31] In 628, he fought in the Battle of Khaybar. In 629, Muhammad sent Amr ibn al-A’as to Zaat-ul-
2 CONC Sallasal, after which, Muhammad sent Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah with reinforcements, including Abu Bakr and Umar, whereupon they attacked and defeated the enemy.[32] In 630, when Muslim armies rushed for the conquest of Mecca, he w
2 CONC as part of that army. Later in 630, he fought in the Battle of Hunayn and the Siege of Ta'if. He was part of the Muslim army that contested the Battle of Tabouk under Muhammad's command and he was reported to have given half of hi
2 CONC s wealth for the preparation of this expedition. He also participated in the farewell Hajj of Muhammad in 632.[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Muhammad
2 CONT When Muhammad died on 8 June 632 Umar initially disbelieved that he was dead.[34] It is said that Umar promised to strike the head of any man who would say that Muhammad died. Umar said: "He has not died but rather he has gone to 
2 CONC his lord just as Moses went, remaining absent from his people for forty nights after which he has returned to them. By Allah, the messenger of Allah will indeed return just as Moses returned (to his people) and he will cut off the
2 CONC  hands and legs of those men who claimed he has died."[35] Abu Bakr then publicly spoke to the community in the mosque, saying:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Whoever worshiped Muhammad, let them know that Muhammad has died, and whoever worshiped Allah, let them know that Allah is alive and never dies."
2 CONT
2 CONT [36] Abū Bakr then recited these verses from the Qur'an:
2 CONT "Muhammad is but a messenger; messengers (the like of whom) have passed away before him. If, then, he dies or is killed, will you turn back on your heel?"
2 CONT
2 CONT [36] Hearing this, Umar fell on his knees in sorrow and acceptance. Sunni Muslims say that this denial of Muhammad's death was occasioned by his deep love for him.[34]
2 CONT
2 CONT Foundation of the caliphate
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikisource has original text related to this article:
2 CONT Letter to Usama bin Zaid
2 CONT Umar's political capacity first manifested as the architect of the caliphate after Muhammad died on 8 June 632.[37] While the funeral of Muhammad was being arranged a group of Muhammad's followers who were natives of Medina, the A
2 CONC nsar (helpers), organised a meeting on the outskirts of the city, effectively locking out those companions known as Muhajirs (The Emigrants) including Umar.[37] Umar found out about this meeting at Saqifah Bani Saadah, and, taking
2 CONC  with him two other Muhajirs, Abu Bakr and Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, proceeded to the meeting, presumably to head off the Ansars' plans for political separation. Arriving at the meeting, Umar was faced with a unified community of
2 CONC  tribes from the Ansar who refused to accept the leadership of the Muhajirs.[37] However, Umar was undeterred in his belief the caliphate should be under the control of the Muhajirs.[38] Though the Khazraj were in disagreement, Um
2 CONC ar, after strained negotiations lasting one or two days, brilliantly divided the Ansar into their old warring factions of Aws and Khazraj tribes. Umar resolved the divisions by placing his hand on that of Abu Bakr as a unity candi
2 CONC date for those gathered in the Saqifah. Others at the Saqifah followed suit, with the exception of the Khazraj tribe and their leader, Sa'd ibn 'Ubada, who were ostracized as a result. The Khazraj tribe is said to have posed no si
2 CONC gnificant threat as there were sufficient men of war from the Medinan tribes such as the Banu Aws to immediately organize them into a military bodyguard for Abu Bakr.[37]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilferd Madelung summarises Umar's contribution:[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar judged the outcome of the Saqifa assembly to be a falta [translated by Madelung as 'a precipitate and ill-considered deal'[40]] because of the absence of most of the prominent Muhajirun, including the Prophet's own family and
2 CONC  clan, whose participation he considered vital for any legitimate consultation (shura, mashwara). It was, he warned the community, to be no precedent for the future. Yet he also defended the outcome, claiming that the Muslims were
2 CONC  longing for Abu Bakr as for no one else. He apologized, moreover, that the Muhajirun present were forced to press for an immediate oath of allegiance since the Ansar could not have been trusted to wait for a legitimate consultati
2 CONC on and might have proceeded to elect one of their own after the departure of the Mekkans. Another reason for Umar to censure the Saqifa meeting as a falta was no doubt its turbulent and undignified end, as he and his followers jum
2 CONC ped upon the sick Khazraji leader Sa'd bin Ubada in order to teach him a lesson, if not to kill him, for daring to challenge the sole right of Quraysh to rule. This violent break-up of the meeting indicates, moreover, that the Ans
2 CONC ar cannot all have been swayed by the wisdom and eloquence of Abu Bakr's speech and have accepted him as the best choice for the succession, as suggested by Caetani. There would have been no sense in beating up the Khazraji chief 
2 CONC if everybody had come around to swearing allegiance to Umar's candidate. A substantial number of the Ansar, presumably of Khazraj in particular, must have refused to follow the lead of the Muhajirun.[39]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to various Twelver Shia sources and Madelung,[41][42] Umar and Abu Bakr had in effect mounted a political coup against Ali at the Saqifah.[37] According to one version of narrations in primary sources, Umar and Abu Bakr 
2 CONC are also said to have used force to try to secure the allegiance from Ali and his party. It has been reported in mainly Persian historical sources written 300 years later, such as in the History of al-Tabari, that after Ali's refu
2 CONC sal to pay homage, Abu Bakr sent Umar with an armed contingent to Fatimah's house where Ali and his supporters are said to have gathered. Umar is reported to have warned those in the House that unless Ali succumbed to Abu Bakr, he
2 CONC  would set the House on fire[38][page needed] and under these circumstances Ali was forced to capitulate. This version of events, fully accepted by Shia scholars, is generally rejected by Sunni scholars who, in view of other repor
2 CONC ts in their literature, believe that Ali gave an oath of alliance to Abu Bakr without any grievance. But then other Sunni and Shia sources say that Ali did not swear allegiance to Abu Bakr after his election but six months later a
2 CONC fter the death of his wife Fatimah putting into question al-Tabari's account. Either way the Sunni and the Shia accounts both accept that Ali felt that Abu Bakr should have informed him before going into the meeting with the Ansar
2 CONC  and that Ali did swear allegiance to Abu Bakr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Western scholars tend to agree that Ali believed he had a clear mandate to succeed Muhammad,[citation needed] but offer differing views as to the extent of use of force by Umar in an attempt to intimidate Ali and his supporters. F
2 CONC or instance, Madelung discounts the possibility of the use of force and argues that:
2 CONT
2 CONT Isolated reports of use of force against Ali and Banu Hashim who unanimously refused to swear allegiance for six months are probably to be discounted. Abu Bakr no doubt was wise enough to restrain Umar from any violence against th
2 CONC em, well realizing that this would inevitably provoke the sense of solidarity of the majority of Abdul Mannaf whose acquiescence he needed.[43] His policy was rather not isolating Banu Hashim as far as possible.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Tom Holland, Umar's historicity is beyond dispute.[44] An Armenian bishop writing a decade or so after Qadisiyya describes Umar as a "mighty potentate coordinating the advance of the sons of Ismael from the depths of 
2 CONC the desert".[44][45] Tom Holland writes "What added incomparably to his prestige, was that his earth-shaking qualities as a generalissimo were combined with the most distinctive cast of virtues. Rather than ape the manner of a Cae
2 CONC sar, as the Ghassanid kings had done, he drew on the example of a quite different kind of Christian. Umar's threadbare robes, his diet of bread, salt and water, and his rejection of worldly riches would have reminded anyone from t
2 CONC he desert reaches beyond Palestine of a very particular kind of person. Monks out in the Judaean desert had long been casting themselves as warriors of God. The achievement of Umar was to take such language to a literal and previo
2 CONC usly unimaginable extreme."[44]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Bakr's era
2 CONT Due to the delicate political situation in Arabia[vague], Umar initially opposed military operations against the rebel tribes there,[citation needed] hoping to gain their support in the event of an invasion by the Romans or the Pe
2 CONC rsians. Later, however, he came to agree with Abu Bakr's strategy to crush the rebellion by force. By late 632 CE, Khalid ibn Walid had successfully united Arabia after consecutive victories against the rebels. During his own reig
2 CONC n later, Umar would mostly adopt the policy of avoiding wars and consolidating his power in the incorporated lands rather than expanding his empire through continuous warfare.[46]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar advised Abu Bakr to compile the Quran in the form of a book after 300 huffāẓ (memorizers) of the Quran died in the Battle of Yamamah.[47]
2 CONT
2 CONT Appointment as a caliph
2 CONT Abu Bakr appointed Umar as his successor before dying in 634 CE.[48] Due to his strict and autocratic nature, Umar was not a very popular figure among the notables of Medina and members of Majlis al Shura; accordingly, high-rankin
2 CONC g companions of Abu Bakr attempted to discourage him from naming Umar.[49][50] Nevertheless, Abu Bakr decided to make Umar his successor. Umar was well known for his extraordinary willpower, intelligence, political astuteness, imp
2 CONC artiality, justice, and care for the poor.[51] Abu Bakr is reported to have said to the high-ranking advisers:
2 CONT
2 CONT His (Umar's) strictness was there because of my softness when the weight of Caliphate will be over his shoulders he will remain no longer strict. If I will be asked by God to whom I have appointed my successor, I will tell him tha
2 CONC t I have appointed the best man among your men.[52]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Bakr was aware of Umar's power and ability to succeed him. His was perhaps one of the smoothest transitions of power from one authority to another in the Muslim lands.[53] Before his death, Abu Bakr called Uthman to write his 
2 CONC will in which he declared Umar his successor. In his will he instructed Umar to continue the conquests on Iraqi and Syrian fronts.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Caliphate
2 CONT Initial challenges
2 CONT Even though almost all of the Muslims had given their pledge of loyalty to Umar, he was feared more than loved. According to Muhammad Husayn Haykal, the first challenge for Umar was to win over his subjects and the members of Majl
2 CONC is al Shura.[54]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was a gifted orator, and he used his ability to improve his reputation among the people.[55]
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad Husayn Haykal wrote that Umar's stress was on the well-being of the poor and underprivileged.[56] In addition to this, Umar, in order to improve his reputation and relation with the Banu Hashim, the tribe of Ali, delivere
2 CONC d to the latter his disputed estates in Khayber. He followed Abu Bakr's decision over the disputed land of Fidak, continuing to treat it as state property. In the Ridda wars, thousands of prisoners from rebel and apostate tribes w
2 CONC ere taken away as slaves during the expeditions. Umar ordered a general amnesty for the prisoners, and their immediate emancipation.[57] This made Umar quite popular among the Bedouin tribes. With the necessary public support on h
2 CONC is side, Umar took the bold decision of recalling Khalid ibn Walid from supreme command on the Roman front.[58]
2 CONT
2 CONT Political and civil administration
2 CONT The government of Umar was a unitary government, where the sovereign political authority was the caliph.[citation needed] The empire of Umar was divided into provinces and some autonomous territories, e.g., Azerbaijan and Armenia,
2 CONC  that had accepted the suzerainty of the caliphate.[citation needed] The provinces were administered by the provincial governors or Wali, personally and fastidiously selected by Umar. Provinces were further divided into about 100 
2 CONC districts. Each district or main city was under the charge of a junior governor or Amir, usually appointed by Umar himself, but occasionally also appointed by the provincial governor. Other officers at the provincial level were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Katib, the Chief Secretary.
2 CONT Katib-ud-Diwan, the Military Secretary.
2 CONT Sahib-ul-Kharaj, the Revenue Collector.
2 CONT Sahib-ul-Ahdath, the Police chief.
2 CONT Sahib-Bait-ul-Mal, the Treasury Officer.
2 CONT Qadi, the Chief Judge.
2 CONT In some districts there were separate military officers, though the Wali was, in most cases, the Commander-in-chief of the army quartered in the province.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Every appointment was made in writing. At the time of appointment an instrument of instructions was issued with a view to regulating the Wali's conduct. On assuming office, the Wali was required to assemble the people in the main 
2 CONC mosque, and read the instrument of instructions before them.[59]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar's general instructions to his officers were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Remember, I have not appointed you as commanders and tyrants over the people. I have sent you as leaders instead, so that the people may follow your example. Give the Muslims their rights and do not beat them lest they become abus
2 CONC ed. Do not praise them unduly, lest they fall into the error of conceit. Do not keep your doors shut in their faces, lest the more powerful of them eat up the weaker ones. And do not behave as if you were superior to them, for tha
2 CONC t is tyranny over them.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Various other strict codes of conduct were to be obeyed by the governors and state officials. The principal officers were required to travel to Mecca on the occasion of the Hajj, during which people were free to present any compla
2 CONC int against them. In order to minimize the chances of corruption, Umar made it a point to pay high salaries to the staff.[citation needed] Provincial governors received as much as five to seven thousand dirham annually besides the
2 CONC ir shares of the spoils of war (if they were also the commander in chief of the army of their sector).[citation needed] Under Umar the empire was divided into the following provinces:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mecca (Arabia)
2 CONT Medina (Arabia)
2 CONT Basra (Iraq)
2 CONT Kufa (Iraq)
2 CONT Jazira, in the upper reaches of the Tigris and Euphrates
2 CONT Syria
2 CONT Iliyā' (إلياء) (Palestine)
2 CONT Ramlah (Palestine)
2 CONT Upper Egypt
2 CONT Lower Egypt
2 CONT Khorasan (Persia)
2 CONT Azerbaijan (Persia)
2 CONT Fars (Persia)
2 CONT Umar was first to establish a special department for the investigation of complaints against the officers of the State. This department acted as the Administrative court, where the legal proceedings were personally led by Umar.[60
2 CONC ] The department was under the charge of Muhammad ibn Maslamah, one of Umar's most trusted men. In important cases Muhammad ibn Maslamah was deputed by Umar to proceed to the spot, investigate the charge and take action. Sometimes
2 CONC  an Inquiry Commission was constituted to investigate the charge. On occasion, the officers against whom complaints were received were summoned to Medina, and charged in Umar's administrative court. Umar was known for this intelli
2 CONC gence service through which he made his officials accountable.[61] This service was also said to have inspired fear in his subjects.[62]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was a pioneer in some affairs:
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was the first to introduce the public ministry system, where the records of officials and soldiers were kept. He also kept a record system for messages he sent to Governors and heads of state.
2 CONT He was the first to appoint police forces to keep civil order.
2 CONT He was the first to discipline the people when they became disordered.[63]
2 CONT Another important aspect of Umar's rule was that he forbade any of his governors and agents from engaging in any sort of business dealings whilst in a position of power. An agent of Umar by the name of Al Harith ibn K'ab ibn Wahb 
2 CONC was once found to have extra money beyond his salary and Umar enquired about his wealth. Al Harith replied that he had some money and he engaged in trade with it. Umar said: By Allah, we did not send you to engage in trade! and he
2 CONC  took from him the profits he had made.[64]
2 CONT
2 CONT Canals
2 CONT Since Medina, with a rapidly growing population, was at risk of recurring famines when crops were lacking, Umar sought to facilitate the import of grain. He ordered the building of a canal connecting the Nile to the Red Sea and an
2 CONC  improvement of port infrastructure on the Arabian coast. When Basra was established during Umar's rule, he started building a nine-mile canal from the Tigris to the new city for irrigation and drinking water.[65] Al-Tabari report
2 CONC s that Utba ibn Ghazwan built the first canal from the Tigris River to the site of Basra when the city was in the planning stage.[citation needed] After the city was built, Umar appointed Abu Musa Ashaari (17-29/638 – 650) as its 
2 CONC first governor.[citation needed] He began building two important canals, the al-Ubulla and the Ma'qil, linking Basra with the Tigris River. These two canals were the basis for the agricultural development for the whole Basra regio
2 CONC n and used for drinking water. Umar also adopted a policy of assigning barren lands to those who undertook to cultivate them. This policy continued during the Umayyad period and resulted in the cultivation of large areas of barren
2 CONC  lands through the construction of irrigation canals by the state and by individuals.[66]
2 CONT
2 CONT Reforms
2 CONT Main articles: Reforms of Umar's era and Covenant of Umar I
2 CONT Under Umar's leadership, the empire expanded; accordingly, he began to build a political structure that would hold together the vast territory. He undertook many administrative reforms and closely oversaw public policy, establishi
2 CONC ng an advanced administration for the newly conquered lands, including several new ministries and bureaucracies, and ordered a census of all the Muslim territories. During his rule, the garrison cities (amsar) of Basra and Kufa we
2 CONC re founded or expanded. In 638, he extended and renovated the Masjid al-Haram (Grand Mosque) in Mecca and al-Masjid al-Nabawi (Mosque of the Prophet) in Medina.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar also ordered the expulsion to Syria and Iraq of the Christian and Jewish communities of Najran and Khaybar. He also permitted Jewish families to resettle in Jerusalem, which had previously been barred from all Jews.[67] He is
2 CONC sued orders that these Christians and Jews should be treated well and allotted them the equivalent amount of land in their new settlements. Umar also forbade non-Muslims from residing in the Hejaz for longer than three days.[68][p
2 CONC age needed][69] He was first to establish the army as a state department.
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was founder of Fiqh, or Islamic jurisprudence.[70] He is regarded by Sunni Muslims as one of the greatest Faqih, and, as such, he started the process of codifying Islamic Law.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 641, he established Bayt al-mal, a financial institution and started annual allowances for the Muslims. As a leader, Umar was known for his simple, austere lifestyle. Rather than adopt the pomp and display affected by the ruler
2 CONC s of the time, he continued to live much as he had when Muslims were poor and persecuted.[citation needed] In 638, his fourth year as caliph and the seventeenth year since the Hijra, he decreed that the Islamic calendar should be 
2 CONC counted from the year of the Hijra of Muhammad from Mecca to Medina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visit to Jerusalem in 637 CE
2 CONT Umar's visit to Jerusalem is documented in several sources. A recently discovered Judeo-Arabic text has disclosed the following anecdote:[67]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Umar ordered Gentiles and a group of Jews to sweep the area of the Temple Mount. Umar oversaw the work. The Jews who had come sent letters to the rest of the Jews in Palestine and informed them that Umar had permitted resettlemen
2 CONC t of Jerusalem by Jews. Umar, after some consultation, permitted seventy Jewish households to return. They returned to live in the southern part of the city, i.e., the Market of the Jews. (Their aim was to be near the water of Sil
2 CONC wan and the Temple Mount and its gates). Then the Commander Umar granted them this request. The seventy families moved to Jerusalem from Tiberias and the area around it with their wives and children."
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also reported in the name of the Alexandrian Bishop Eutychius (932–940 CE) that the rock known as the Temple Mount had been a place of ruins as far back as the time of the Empress Helena, mother of Constantine the Great, who
2 CONC  built churches in Jerusalem. "The Byzantines," he said, "had deliberately left the ancient site of the Temple as it was, and had even thrown rubbish on it, so that a great heap of rubble formed." It was only when Umar marched int
2 CONC o Jerusalem with an army that he asked Kaab, who was Jewish before he converted to Islam, "Where do you advise me to build a place of worship?" Kaab indicated the Temple Rock, now a gigantic heap of ruins from the temple of Jupite
2 CONC r.[71] The Jews, Kaab explained, had briefly won back their old capital a quarter of a century before (when Persians overran Syria and Palestine), but they had not had time to clear the site of the Temple, for the Rums (Byzantines
2 CONC ) had recaptured the city. It was then that Umar ordered the rubbish on the Ṣakhra (rock) to be removed by the Nabataeans, and after three showers of heavy rain had cleansed the Rock, he instituted prayers there. To this day, the 
2 CONC place is known as ḳubbat es ṣakhra, the Dome of the Rock.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to lexicographer David ben Abraham al-Fasi (died before 1026 CE), the Muslim conquest of Palestine brought relief to the country's Jewish citizens, who had previously been barred by the Byzantines from praying on the Tem
2 CONC ple Mount.[72]
2 CONT
2 CONT Military expansion
2 CONT Main article: Military conquests of Umar's era
2 CONT vte
2 CONT Wars of Caliph Umar
2 CONT The military conquests were partially terminated between 638 and 639 during the years of great famine in Arabia and plague in the Levant. During his reign the Levant, Egypt, Cyrenaica, Tripolitania, Fezzan, Eastern Anatolia, almos
2 CONC t the whole of the Sassanid Persian Empire including Bactria, Persia, Azerbaijan, Armenia, Caucasus and Makran were annexed to the Rashidun Caliphate. According to one estimate more than 4,050 cities were captured during these mil
2 CONC itary conquests.[73] Prior to his death in 644, Umar had ceased all military expeditions apparently to consolidate his rule in recently conquered Roman Egypt and the newly conquered Sassanid Empire (642–644). At his death in Novem
2 CONC ber 644, his rule extended from present day Libya in the west to the Indus river in the east and the Oxus river in the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT Great famine
2 CONT In 638 CE, Arabia fell into severe drought followed by a famine. Soon after, the reserves of food at Medina began to run out. Umar ordered caravans of supplies from Syria and Iraq, and personally supervised their distribution. His
2 CONC  actions saved countless lives throughout Arabia.[74] The first governor to respond was Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, the governor of Syria and supreme commander of the Rashidun army.[75]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, Abu Ubaidah paid a personal visit to Medina and acted as an officer of disaster management, which was headed personally by Umar. For internally displaced people, Umar hosted a dinner every night at Medina, which according t
2 CONC o one estimate, had attendance of more than a hundred thousand people.[76]
2 CONT
2 CONT Great plague
2 CONT While famine was ending in Arabia, many districts in Syria and Palestine were devastated by plague. While Umar was on his way to visit Syria, at Elat, he was received by Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, governor of Syria, who informed h
2 CONC im about the plague and its intensity, and suggested that Umar go back to Medina. Umar tried to persuade Abu Ubaidah to come with him to Medina, but he declined to leave his troops in that critical situation. Abu Ubaidah died in 6
2 CONC 39 of the plague, which also cost the lives of 25,000 Muslims in Syria. After the plague had weakened, in late 639, Umar visited Syria for political and administrative re-organization, as most of the veteran commanders and governo
2 CONC rs had died of the plague.[77]
2 CONT
2 CONT Welfare state
2 CONT To be close to the poor, Umar lived in a simple mud hut without doors and walked the streets every evening. After consulting with the poor, Umar established the first welfare state, Bayt al-mal.[78][79][80] The Bayt al-mal aided t
2 CONC he Muslim and non-Muslim poor, needy, elderly, orphans, widows, and the disabled. The Bayt al-mal ran for hundreds of years, from the Rashidun Caliphate in the 7th century through the Umayyad period (661–750) and well into the Abb
2 CONC asid era. Umar also introduced a child benefit and pensions for the children and the elderly.[81][82][83][84]
2 CONT
2 CONT Free trade
2 CONT Local populations of Jews and Christians, persecuted as religious minorities and taxed heavily to finance the Byzantine–Sassanid Wars, often aided Muslims to take over their lands from the Byzantines and Persians, resulting in exc
2 CONC eptionally speedy conquests.[85][86] As new areas were attached to the Caliphate, they also benefited from free trade, while trading with other areas in the Caliphate (to encourage commerce, in Islam trade is not taxed, but wealth
2 CONC  is subject to the zakat).[87] Since the Constitution of Medina, drafted by Muhammad, the Jews and the Christians continued to use their own laws in the Caliphate and had their own judges.[88][89][90]
2 CONT
2 CONT ASSASSINATION
2 CONT In 644, Umar was assassinated by a Persian slave named Abu Lu'lu'a Firuz. His motivation for the assassination is not clear, but medieval sources attribute it to a tax dispute with his Arab master al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba.[92]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some historical accounts, Abu Lu'lu'a was a Zoroastrian from Nahavand (Iran), though other reports describe him as a Christian.[93] A highly skilled joiner and blacksmith,[94] Abu Lu'lu'a was probably taken captive by
2 CONC  his master al-Mughira in the Battle of Nahavand (642) and subsequently brought to Arabia, where he may also have converted to Islam.[95] Other historical sources report that he was rather taken captive by al-Mughira in the Battle
2 CONC  of al-Qadisiyya (636), or that he was sold to al-Mughira by Hurmuzān, an ex-Sassanid military officer who had been working for Umar as an adviser after his own capture by the Muslims.[96] Although Medina was generally off-limits 
2 CONC to the ʿajam (non-Arabs) under Umar's reign, Abu Lu'lu'a was exceptionally allowed to enter the capital of the early caliphate, being sent there by al-Mughira to serve the caliph.[97]
2 CONT
2 CONT When al-Mughira forced Abu Lu'lu'a to pay a kharāj tax of two dirhams a day,[98] Abu Lu'lu'a turned to Umar to protest this tax. However, Umar refused to lift the tax, thus provoking Abu Lu'lu'a's rage.[99] Although this is the re
2 CONC ason given by most historical accounts for Abu Lu'lu'a's assassination of Umar,[100] Umar's biased policies against non-Arab captives may also have played a prominent role.[101] One day when Umar was leading the congregational pra
2 CONC yer in the mosque of Medina, Abu Lu'lu'a stabbed him with a double-bladed dagger.[102] There are different versions of how this happened: according to one version, he also killed Kulayb ibn al-Bukayr al-Laythi who was behind Umar,
2 CONC [103] while in another version he stabbed thirteen people who tried to restrain him.[104] According to some accounts, the caliph died on the same day, while other accounts maintain that he died three days later.[94] In any case, U
2 CONC mar died of his wounds on Wednesday 26 Dhu al-Hijja of the Islamic year 23 (6 November 644 according to the Gregorian calendar).[105]
2 CONT
2 CONT Some historical sources report that Abu Lu'lu'a was taken prisoner and executed for his assassination of Umar, while other sources claim that he committed suicide.[94] After Abu Lu'lu'a's death, his daughter was killed by Ubayd Al
2 CONC lah ibn Umar, one of Umar's sons. Acting upon the claim of one man (either Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf or Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr) that they had been seen conspiring with Abu Lu'lu'a while he was holding the double-bladed dagger, Uba
2 CONC yd Allah also killed Hurmuzān (Umar's Persian military adviser), and Jufayna, a Christian man from al-Hira (Iraq) who had been taken to Medina to serve as a private tutor to a family in Medina.[106] After Ubayd Allah was detained 
2 CONC for these murders, he threatened to kill all foreign captives residing in Medina, as well as some others. Although Ubayd Allah may have been encouraged by his sister Hafsa bint Umar to avenge their father's death, his murder of Hu
2 CONC rmuzān and Jufayna was likely the result of a mental breakdown rather than of a true conspiracy. It was regarded by his peers as a crime rather than as a legitimate act of retaliation.[107]
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 20th-century scholarship it was sometimes supposed that Abu Lu'lu'a had really been an instrument in the hands of a conspiracy, though not a conspiracy led by Hurmuzān, but rather one led by Ali, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, a
2 CONC nd Talha ibn Ubayd Allah. These men, who according to the historical sources were appointed by Umar himself as members of the council who would elect the next caliph, were thought by scholars to have conspired to overthrow Umar's 
2 CONC reign and to put Ali in his place.[108] This hypothesis, however, is rejected by more recent scholars.[109] Nevertheless, while Ubayd Allah was subsequently acquitted of his crimes by Umar's successor Uthman (r. 644–656), who cons
2 CONC idered the execution of Ubayd Allah an excessive measure in view of his father's recent assassination,[110] Ali, among others, did protest against this and vowed to apply the regular punishment for murder if he were ever to be cal
2 CONC iph.[111]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar was buried at the Green Dome in al-Masjid al-Nabawi alongside Muhammad and the caliph Abu Bakr, by the permission of Aisha given to his son Abdullah ibn Umar on Umar's request.[112]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aftermath
2 CONT On his deathbed, Umar vacillated on his succession. However, it has been reported that he said that if Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah, Khalid ibn Walid or Salim, the mawla and freed Persian slave, were alive he would have appointed one
2 CONC  of them his successor.[citation needed] Umar finally appointed a committee of six persons to choose a caliph from amongst them: Abdur Rahman bin Awf, Saad ibn Abi Waqqas, Talha ibn Ubaidullah, Uthman ibn Affan, Ali ibn Abi Talib 
2 CONC and Zubayr ibn al-Awwam.[113]
2 CONT
2 CONT All six are among the ten to whom Paradise was promised according to Sunnis.[114] The only one out of the 'famous ten' left out of the committee who was still alive at the time was Saeed ibn Zaid, the cousin and brother-in-law of 
2 CONC Umar. He was excluded on the basis of being related by blood and of the same tribe as Umar. Umar had a policy of not appointing anyone related to him to a position of authority even if they were qualified by his standards.[115]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar appointed a band of fifty armed soldiers to protect the house where the meeting was proceeding. Until the appointment of the next caliph, Umar appointed a notable Sahabi and mawla, Suhayb ar-Rumi (Suhayb the Roman), as a care
2 CONC taker caliph.[citation needed] While the meeting for selection of a caliph was proceeding, Abdulrehman ibn Abu Bakr and Abdur Rahman bin Awf revealed that they saw the dagger used by Abu Lu'lu'a, the assassin of Umar. A night befo
2 CONC re Umar's assassination, reported Abdur Rahman bin Awf, he saw Hurmuzan, Jafina and Abu Lu'lu'a, while they were suspiciously discussing something.[citation needed] Surprised by his presence, the dagger fell; it was the same two-s
2 CONC ided dagger used in the assassination. Abdulrehman ibn Abu Bakr, son of the late caliph Abu Bakr, confirmed that, a few days before Umar's assassination, he saw this dagger in Hurmuzan's possession. After this revelation, it seeme
2 CONC d clear that it had been planned by the Persians residing in Medina. Infuriated by this, Umar's younger son Ubaidullah ibn Umar sought to kill all the Persians in Medina.[citation needed] He killed Hurmuzan, Jafinah, and the daugh
2 CONC ter of Umar's assassin Abu Lu'lu'a, who is believed to have been a Muslim. Ubaidullah was intercepted by the people of Medina, who prevented him from continuing the massacre. Amr ibn al-Aas is said to have intercepted him and conv
2 CONC inced him to hand over his sword. The murder of Jafinah enraged Saad ibn Abi Waqqas, his foster brother, and he assaulted Ubaidullah ibn Umar; again the companions intervened. When Umar was informed about the incident, he ordered 
2 CONC Ubaidullah imprisoned, and that the next caliph should decide his fate.[116]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar died on 3 November 644; on 7 November Uthman succeeded him as caliph. After prolonged negotiations, the tribunal decided to give blood money to the victims, and released Umar's son Ubaidullah on the ground that, after the tra
2 CONC gedy of Umar's assassination, people would be further infuriated by the execution of his son the very next day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Physical appearance
2 CONT Umar was strong, fit, athletic and good at wrestling. He is said to have participated in the wrestling matches on the occasion of the annual fair of Ukaz.[117] From first hand accounts of his physical appearance Umar is said to be
2 CONC  vigorous, robust and a very tall man; in markets he would tower above the people. The front part of his head was bald, always A'sara Yusran (working with two hands),[118] both his eyes were black, with yellow skin; however, ibn S
2 CONC a'ad in his book stated that he never knew that Umar had yellow skin, except for a certain part of Umar's life where his color changed due to his frequent consumption of oil.[119] Others[who?] say he had reddish-white skin.[citati
2 CONC on needed] His teeth were ashnabul asnan (very white shining). He would always color his beard and take care of his hair using a type of plant.[119][120]
2 CONT
2 CONT The early Muslim historians Ibn Saad and al-Hakim mention that Abu Miriam Zir, a native of Kufa, described Umar as being "advanced in years, bald, of a tawny colour – a left handed man, tall and towering above the people".[117] Um
2 CONC ar's eldest son Abdullah described his father as "a man of fair complexion, a ruddy tint prevailing, tall, bald and grey".[citation needed] Historian Salima bin al-Akwa'a said that "Umar was ambidextrous, he could use both his han
2 CONC ds equally well". On the authority of Abu Raja al-U'taridi, Ibn Asakir records that "Umar was a man tall, stout, very bald, very ruddy with scanty hair on the cheeks, his moustaches large, and the ends thereof reddish".[117]
2 CONT
2 CONT Assessments and legacy
2 CONT Political legacy
2 CONT Umar was the first caliph to adopt the title amir al-mu'minin.[121] Umar was one of Muhammad's chief advisers. After Muhammad's passing, it was Umar who reconciled the Medinan Muslims to accept Abu Bakr, a Meccan, as the caliph.[1
2 CONC 22] During Abu Bakr's era, he actively participated as his secretary and main adviser.[123] After succeeding Abu Bakr as caliph, Umar won over the hearts of Bedouin tribes by emancipating all their prisoners and slaves taken durin
2 CONC g the Ridda wars.[124]
2 CONT
2 CONT He built up an efficient administrative structure that held together his vast realm. He organized an effective intelligence network, one of the reasons for his strong grip on his bureaucracy.[125]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar never appointed governors for more than two years, for they might amass too much local power. He dismissed his most successful general, Khalid ibn Walid, because he wanted people to know that it is Allah who grants victory, a
2 CONC nd to counter the cult of personality that had built up around Khalid, for the sake of the Muslim faith.[126]
2 CONT
2 CONT He would patrol the streets of Medina with a whip in his hand, ready to punish any offenders he might come across. It is said that Umar's whip was feared more than the sword of another man. But with all of this, he was also known 
2 CONC for being kindhearted, answering the needs of the fatherless and widows.[127]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar's swift imposition of justice against his governors for misdeeds made even powerful governors such as Muawiyah scared of him. Ali ibn Abu Talib, during the later rule of Uthman ibn Affan, wanted Uthman to be more strict with 
2 CONC his governors, saying, "I adjure you by God, do you know that Mu'awiyah was more afraid of Umar than was Umar's own servant Yarfa?"[128]
2 CONT
2 CONT Under Umar's rule, in order to promote strict discipline, Arab soldiers were settled outside of cities, between the desert and cultivated lands in special garrison towns known as "amsar". Known examples of such settlements are Bas
2 CONC ra and Kufa, in Iraq, and Fustat south of what would later become Cairo. His soldiers were forbidden to own land outside of Arabia. There were restrictions on their right to seize buildings and other immovable things usually thoug
2 CONC ht of as prizes of war. Movable spoils were shared with the people of the umma, regardless of their social stratum.[129]
2 CONT
2 CONT A modern researcher writes about this:[130]
2 CONT
2 CONT He used to monitor public policy very closely, and had kept the needs of the public central to his leadership approach. As second caliph of Islam, he refused to chop off the hands of thieves because he felt he had fallen short of 
2 CONC his responsibility to provide meaningful employment to all his subjects. As a ruler of a vast kingdom, his vision was to ensure that every one in his kingdom should sleep on a full stomach.
2 CONT
2 CONT If a dog dies hungry on the banks of the River Euphrates, Umar will be responsible for dereliction of duty.
2 CONT
2 CONT — (Umar)
2 CONT He also knew that just having a vision is not enough unless it is supported by effective strategies. He didn't only have a vision; he truly transformed his vision into actions. For example, to ensure that nobody sleeps hungry in h
2 CONC is empire, he used to walk through the streets almost every night to see if there is any one needy or ill.
2 CONT
2 CONT In The Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire, Gibbon refers to Umar in the following terms:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Yet the abstinence and humility of Umar were not inferior to the virtues of Abubeker; his food consisted of barley bread or dates; his drink was water; he preached in a gown that was torn or tattered in twelve places; and a Persi
2 CONC an satrap who paid his homage to the conqueror, found him asleep among the beggars on the steps of the mosque of Medina."[131]
2 CONT
2 CONT His rule was one of the few moments in the history of Islam where Muslims were united as a single community. Abdullah ibn Masʿud would often weep whenever the subject of Umar was brought up. He said: "Umar was a fortress of Islam.
2 CONC  People would enter Islam and not leave. When he died, the fortress was breached and now people are going out of Islam".[132] Abu Ubaidah ibn al-Jarrah before Umar died famously said: "If Umar dies, Islam would be weakened". Peopl
2 CONC e asked him why and his reply was "You will see what I am speaking about if you survive."[132] His greatest achievement from a religious perspective was the compilation of the Qur'an.[133] This had not been done during the time of
2 CONC  Muhammad. However, during the Battle of Yamama a great number of the memorizers of the Quran perished in the battle. On the advice of Umar, Abu Bakr tasked Zayd ibn Thabit with the momentous task of compiling the Quran into a sin
2 CONC gle Book.[47]
2 CONT
2 CONT Military legacy
2 CONT Along with Khalid ibn Walid, Umar was influential in the Ridda wars.[134]
2 CONT
2 CONT One strategic success was his sundering of the Byzantine-Sassanid alliance in 636, when Emperor Heraclius and Emperor Yazdegerd III allied against their common enemy.[citation needed] He was lucky in that the Persian Emperor Yazde
2 CONC gerd III couldn't synchronize with Heraclius as planned. Umar fully availed himself of the opportunity by inducing the Byzantines to act prematurely.[citation needed] This was contrary to the orders of Emperor Heraclius, who presu
2 CONC mably wanted a coordinated attack along with the Persians. Umar did this by sending reinforcements to the Roman front in the Battle of Yarmouk, with instructions that they should appear in the form of small bands, one after the ot
2 CONC her, giving the impression of a continuous stream of reinforcements that finally lured the Byzantines to an untimely battle.[citation needed] On the other hand, Yazdegerd III was engaged in negotiations that further gave Umar time
2 CONC  to transfer his troops from Syria to Iraq. These troops proved decisive in the Battle of Qadisiyyah.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT His strategy resulted in a Muslim victory at the Second Battle of Emesa in 638, where the pro-Byzantine Christian Arabs of Jazira, aided by the Byzantine Emperor, made an unexpected flanking movement and laid siege to Emesa (Homs)
2 CONC .[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar issued an order to invade the very homeland of the Christian Arab forces besieging Emesa, the Jazirah. A three-pronged attack against Jazirah was launched from Iraq. To further pressure the Christian Arab armies, Umar instruc
2 CONC ted Saad ibn Abi Waqqas, commander of Muslim forces in Iraq, to send reinforcements to Emesa. Umar himself led reinforcements there from Medina. Under this unprecedented pressure, the Christian Arabs retreated from Emesa before Mu
2 CONC slim reinforcements could arrive. The Muslims annexed Mesopotamia and parts of Byzantine Armenia.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Battle of Nahavand, Umar launched a full-scale invasion of the Sassanid Persian Empire. The invasion was a series of well-coordinated multi-pronged attacks designed to isolate and destroy their targets. Umar launched the
2 CONC  invasion by attacking the very heart of Persia, aiming to isolate Azerbaijan and eastern Persia.[citation needed] This was immediately followed by simultaneous attacks on Azerbaijan and Fars. Next, Sistan and Kirman were captured
2 CONC , thus isolating the stronghold of Persia, the Khurasan. The final expedition was launched against Khurasan, where, after the Battle of Oxus River, the Persian empire ceased to exist, and Yazdegerd III fled to Central Asia.[citati
2 CONC on needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious legacy
2 CONT Sunni views
2 CONT Main article: Sunni view of Umar
2 CONT Umar is remembered by Sunnis as a rigid Muslim of a sound and just disposition in matters of religion; a man they title Fārūq, meaning "leader, jurist and statesman", and the second of the rightly guided caliphs. He patched his cl
2 CONC othes with skin, took buckets on his two shoulders, always riding his donkey without the saddle, rarely laughing and never joking with anyone. On his ring is written the words "Enough is Death as a reminder to you O' Umar".[135] H
2 CONC e did not seek advancement for his own family, but rather sought to advance the interests of the Muslim community, the ummah. According to one of Muhammad's companions, Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud:
2 CONT
2 CONT Umar's submission to Islam was a conquest, his migration was a victory, his Imamate (period of rule) was a blessing, I have seen when we were unable to pray at the Kaabah until Umar submitted, when he submitted to Islam, he fought
2 CONC  them (the pagans) until they left us alone and we prayed.
2 CONT
2 CONT — Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, [136]
2 CONT Shia views
2 CONT Main article: Shia view of Umar
2 CONT Umar is viewed very negatively in the literature of Twelver Shi'a (the main branch of Shia Islam[137][138]) and is often regarded as a usurper of Ali's right to the Caliphate. After the Saqifah assembly chose Abu Bakr as caliph, U
2 CONC mar marched with armed men to Ali's house in order to get the allegiance of Ali and his supporters. Sources indicate that a threat was made to burn Ali's house if he refused, but the encounter ended when Fatimah, wife of Ali, inte
2 CONC rvened.[139] According to the majority of Twelver scholar writings, Fatimah was physically assaulted by Umar, that this caused her to miscarry her child, Muhsin ibn Ali, and led to her death soon after.[140] (see Umar at Fatimah's
2 CONC  house). However, some Twelver scholars, such as Fadhlalla, reject these accounts of physical abuse as a "myth",[141] although Fadlallah mentioned that his speech is a probability, and not a certain reason to reject that event.[14
2 CONC 2][better source needed][143][better source needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Another Shia sect, the Zaidiyyah followers of Zaid ibn Ali, generally has two views about that. Some branches, such as Jaroudiah (Sarhubiyya), don't accept Umar and Abu Bakr as legitimate caliphs. For instance, Jarudiyya believes 
2 CONC that Muhammad appointed Ali and believes that the denial of the Imamate of Ali after Muhammad's passing would lead to infidelity and deviation from the right path.[citation needed] The other view accepts Umar and Abu Bakr as legit
2 CONC imate caliphs, albeit inferior to Ali.[144] According to al-Tabari (and Ibn A'tham),[145] when asked about Abu Bakr and Umar, Zayd ibn Ali replied: "I have not heard anyone in my family renouncing them both nor saying anything but
2 CONC  good about them...when they were entrusted with government they behaved justly with the people and acted according to the Qur'an and the Sunnah."[146][147]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Main article: Family tree of Umar
2 CONT Umar married nine women in his lifetime and had fourteen children: ten sons and four daughters.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives
2 CONT The known wives of Umar are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Zaynab bint Maz'un, she was the mother of Hafsa, Abd Allah and Abd al-Rahman al-Akbar.
2 CONT Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal, she was divorced by Umar. She was the mother of Ubayd Allah and Zayd al-Asghar.
2 CONT Qurayba bint Abi Umayya, divorced by Umar in 628.
2 CONT Jamila bint Thabit, She married Umar about between May 627 and May 628.[148] They had one son, Asim.[149][150][151][148][152]
2 CONT Atiqa bint Zayd, she was married to Umar and had a son named Iyad.
2 CONT Umm Hakim bint al-Harith ibn Hisham, She was married to Umar[153] in 634 and was mother of Fatima.
2 CONT Umm Kulthum bint Ali from this marriage Umar had a son named Zayd and a daughter named Ruqayya. This is, however, the Sunni view. The Shi'a do not accept that such a marriage took place. In fact, even some Sunnis scholars maintain
2 CONC  that Umar's wife Umm Kulthum was actually Abu Bakr's daughter who was raised in Ali's house.[154]
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT The sons of Umar are:[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abd Allah, son of Zaynab bint Maz'un.
2 CONT Abd al-Rahman, son of Zaynab bint Maz'un.
2 CONT Zayd, son of Umm Kulthum bint Ali.[155]
2 CONT Ubayd Allah, son of Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal.
2 CONT Zayd, son of Umm Kulthum bint Jarwal.
2 CONT Asim, son of Jamila bint Thabit.
2 CONT Iyad, son of Atiqa bint Zayd.
2 CONT Abd al-Rahman Abu'l-Mujabbar
2 CONT Abd al-Rahman "Abu Shahmah" ibn Umar
2 CONT Abd Allah
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT The daughters of Umar are:[citation needed]
