1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''4th Umayyad Caliph'''
2 CONT * Reign: 684 – 685 AD
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 1: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marwan_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 2: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marwan_ibn_al-Hakam
2 CONT
2 CONT Source 3: http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marwan_I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan ibn al-Hakam (623–685) (Arabic: مروان بن الحكم‎) was the fourth Umayyad Caliph, who took over the dynasty after Muawiya II abdicated in 684. Marwan's ascension pointed to a shift in the lineage of the Umayyad dynasty from d
2 CONC escendants of Abu Sufyan to those of Hakam, both of whom were grandsons of Umayya (for whom the Umayyad dynasty is named). Hakam was a first cousin of Uthman ibn Affan.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the "Battle of the Camel" Marwan ibn al-Hakam is said to have shot his general Talhah with an arrow to the thigh, resulting in his death. Marwan killed Talha in revenge for Talha's alleged betrayal of the third Caliph Uthma
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was removed from this position by Ali, only to be reappointed by Muawiya I. Marwan was eventually removed from the city when Abdullah ibn Zubayr rebelled against Yazid I. From here, Marwan went to Damascus, where he was made th
2 CONC e caliph after Muawiya II abdicated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan's short reign was marked by a civil war among the Umayyads as well as a war against Abdullah ibn Zubayr who continued to rule over the Hejaz, Iraq, Egypt and parts of Syria. Marwan was able to win the Umayyad civil war, the
2 CONC  result of which was a new Marwanid line of Umayyad caliphs. He was also able to recapture Egypt and Syria from Abdullah, but was not able to completely defeat him. Shi'a hold that none of the Umayyad caliphs were legitimate. See 
2 CONC Succession to Muhammad for more details.
2 CONT
2 CONT -
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th Umayyad Caliph1,2
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 623, d. 685
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	al-Hakim ibn Abûl 'Âs1,3,4 b. circa 599
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Amina bint `Alkama al-Kinaniyya1,3 b. circa 604
2 CONT
2 CONT      Of the Umayyad. Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th Umayyad Caliph was named in full: Marwan bin Al-Hakam bin Abi Al-`As bin Umaiyah.5 Also called Abu `l-Kasim = Father of 'l-Kasim.1 Also called Abu `Abd al-Malik = Father 
2 CONC of 'Abd al-Malik. He was born in 623.5,6 He was the son of al-Hakim ibn Abûl 'Âs and Amina bint `Alkama al-Kinaniyya.1,3,4 Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th Umayyad Caliph married 'A'isha bint Mu`awiya ben al-Mughira, daughte
2 CONC r of Mu`awiya ben al-Mughira, before 646.1 Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th Umayyad Caliph was a loyal follower of the Caliph 'Uthman bin 'Affan.5 'A'isha, the widow of the Prophet Muhammed, rebelled against the Caliphs. Her
2 CONC  resistance ended with her defeat in this battle. In 658 at the Battle of the Camel, Basra (now Az-Zubayr), southeastern Iraq.7 He a participant in 658 at the Battle of the Camel, Basra (now Az-Zubayr), southeastern Iraq.5 He marr
2 CONC ied Aisha bint 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, daughter of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan al-Qurayshi, 3rd Rightly Guided Caliph of Islam and Ruqaiyah bint Muhammad Banu Hâshim, before 659; 1st cousins, 1x removed. Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th 
2 CONC Umayyad Caliph was the fourth Umayyad caliph, and forefather of the Marwanids in 683.5 4th Umayyad Caliph at Damascus, Syria, between 683 and 685.5 He was an old man in poor health when he ascended the throne himself in 684.6 He w
2 CONC as established the standards for weights and measurements.5 He died in 685 at Damascus, Syria, at age 62 years. He died of plague.1,2,5,6 He was able to arrange the succession of his son, 'Abd al-Malik, by eliminating all other co
2 CONC ntenders for the caliphate.6
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1
2 CONT
2 CONT 'A'isha bint Mu`awiya ben al-Mughira b. circa 630
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwân, 5th Umayyad Caliph+ b. c 646, d. Oct 7051,8
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 2
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-'Azîz ibn Marwân, Governor of Egypt+ b. c 650, d. May 7059,4
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad ibn Marwân Banu 'Abd Shams+ b. c 6559
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Aisha bint 'Uthman ibn 'Affan b. circa 617
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT N. N. bint Marwân ibn al-Hakam+ b. c 659
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S187] Royal Genealogy Database, online http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/public/genealogy/
2 CONT
2 CONT [S278] DfAdam, online unknown url, The Line of Ishmael, 82.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S976] Francisco Antonio Doria (e-mail address), Banu-Qasi conjectured line in "Re: Banu-Qasi conjectured line," newsgroup message 2001-06-12 06:42:26 PST.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S1411] Stanley Lane-Pool, The Mohammadan Dynasties, pg. 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S469] Al-Islam.com, online http://www.al-islam.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT [S172] Various Encyclopaedea Britannica.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S862] Various EB CD 2001, Zubayr, Az- (Iraq) .
2 CONT
2 CONT [S862] Various EB CD 2001, 'Abd al-Malik (Umayyad caliph).
2 CONT
2 CONT [S653] PoH, online http://www.friesian.com/
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Marwan I
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan ibn al-Hakam (623 - 685) (árabe: مروان بن الحكم) era el cuarto Califa Omeya, que asumió el control de la dinastía después de que Muawiya II abdicara en 684. La ascensión de Marwan señaló un cambio en el linaje de la dinastí
2 CONC a Omeya al ser descendientes de Abu Sufyan a las de Hakam, ambas quienes eran nietos de Umayya (quién da su nombre a la dinastía). Hakam era primo del Uthman ibn Affan.
2 CONT
2 CONT El profeta Mahoma envió a ambos y a su padre, Hakam ibn al-Aas al exilio, con la orden de nunca volver. Durante el reinado de Uthman, él envió por Marwan y posiblemente por su padre. Marwan se aprovechó de su relación con él calif
2 CONC a y fue designado gobernador de Medina. Un número de historiadores posteriores tales como Ibn Taymiyya y los sunis en general se preguntan sobre la autenticidad de esta historia, la cual no viene de ningún escritor después de la m
2 CONC uerte de Mahoma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante la “batalla del camello” Marwan ibn al-Hakam se dice que disparo una flecha en el muslo al general Talha, resultando en su muerte. Marwan mato a Talha en venganza por la traición alegada a Talha al tercer Califa Uthman.
2 CONT
2 CONT El fue removido de esta posición por Ali, sólo fue repuesto por Muawiya I. Marwan se le quito eventual la ciudad cuando Abdullah ibn Zubayr se rebeló contra Yazid I. De aquí, Marwan fue a Damasco, en donde lo proclamaron califa de
2 CONC spués de que Muawiya II abdicara.
2 CONT
2 CONT El reinado corto de Marwan fue marcado por una guerra civil entre los Omeyas así como una guerra contra Abdullah ibn Zubayr que continuó gobernando sobre el Hejaz, Iraq, Egipto y partes de Siria. Marwan pudo ganar la guerra civil 
2 CONC de los Omeyasd, el resultado de la cual fue una nueva línea de los Califas Omeyas en los Marwanidas. Él pudo también recobrar Egipto y Siria de Abdullah, pero no pudo derrotarlo totalmente.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan ibn al-Hakam (623 - 685) (Arabic: مروان بن الحكم‎) was the fourth Umayyad Caliph, who took over the dynasty after Muawiya II abdicated in 684. Marwan's ascension pointed to a shift in the lineage of the Umayyad dynasty from
2 CONC  descendants of Abu Sufyan to those of Hakam, both of whom were grandsons of Umayya (for whom the Umayyad dynasty is named). Hakam was a first cousin of Uthman ibn Affan.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the "Battle of the Camel" Marwan ibn al-Hakam is said to have shot his general Talha with an arrow to the thigh, resulting in his death. Marwan killed Talha in revenge for Talha's alleged betrayal of the third Caliph Uthman
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was removed from this position by Ali, only to be reappointed by Muawiya I. Marwan was eventually removed from the city when Abdullah ibn Zubayr rebelled against Yazid I. From here, Marwan went to Damascus, where he was made th
2 CONC e caliph after Muawiya II abdicated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan's short reign was marked by a civil war among the Umayyads as well as a war against Abdullah ibn Zubayr who continued to rule over the Hejaz, Iraq, Egypt and parts of Syria. Marwan was able to win the Umayyad civil war, the
2 CONC  result of which was a new Marwanid line of Umayyad caliphs. He was also able to recapture Egypt and Syria from Abdullah, but was not able to completely defeat him. Shi'a hold that none of the Umayyad caliphs were legitimate. See 
2 CONC Succession to Muhammad for more details.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marwan_I Marwan ibn al-Hakam ibn Abu al-'As ibn Umayyah (623 – 7 May 685) (Arabic: مروان بن الحكم بن ابو العاص بن أمية‎) was the fourth Umayyad Caliph,
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aisha bint 'Uthman ibn 'Affan married Marwân I ibn al-Hakam al-Qurayshi, 4th Umayyad Caliph, son of al-Hakim ibn Abûl 'Âs and Amina bint `Alkama al-Kinaniyya, before 659; 1st cousins, 1x removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan I ibn Abu al-Hakim (born 28th March 623), Governor of Medina, later 8th CALIPH OF DAMASCUS (684-85), married his second-cousin once-removed [MOSQ223]A'isha bint 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (see 2 entries earlier). He died 7th May 68
2 CONC 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT [MOSQ222] --- bint Marwan I married [MOSN221] Musa ibn Nuseir al-Bekir (see al-ANDALUS below).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Place of Burial:Immediate Family: Marwân I al-Hakam, 4th Umayyad Caliph Also Known As:	"مروان بن الحكم‎" Birthdate:	March 28, 623 Birthplace:	Damascus, Syria  Death:	Died May 7, 685 in Damascus, Syria Damascus, Syria Son of al-Ha
2 CONC kam bin Abu al-ʻAs and Aminah binte al-Kama al-Kinaniyya Husband of 'Aisha binte ʻUthmān; A'isha binte Mu`awiyah bin al-Mughirah and Ruqayya binte Umar al-Hakam Father of 'Abd al-'Aziz bin Marwân I bin al-Hakam;Amîna binte Marwân 
2 CONC I bin al-Hakam; 'Abd al-Malik I bin Marwân I bin al-Hakam and Muhammad bin Marwân I bin al-Hakam
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation:	Calife, de Damas, Fourth Omayyad Caliph, Enkel Mohammeds Marwan ibn al-Hakam ibn Abu al-'As ibn Umayyah (623 – 7 May 685) (Arabic: مروان بن الحكم بن ابو العاص بن أمية‎) a Sahaba and also was the fourth Umayyad Caliph, 
2 CONC and the cousin of Uthman ibn Affan, who took over the dynasty after Muawiya II abdicated in 684. Marwan's ascension pointed to a shift in the lineage of the Umayyad dynasty from descendants of Abu Sufyan (the "Sufyanids") to those
2 CONC  of Hakam (the "Marwanids"), both of whom were grandsons of Umayya (for whom the Umayyad dynasty is named). Hakam was also the uncle of Uthman ibn Affan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life During the "Battle of the Camel" Marwan ibn al-Hakam is said to have shot his general Talhah with an arrow to the thigh, resulting in his death. Marwan killed Talhah in revenge for his alleged betrayal of the third Caliph, Ut
2 CONC hman. He was removed from this position by Ali, only to be reappointed by Muawiya I. Marwan was eventually removed from the city when Abdullah ibn Zubayr rebelled against Yazid I. From here, Marwan went to Damascus, where he was m
2 CONC ade the caliph afterMuawiya II abdicated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marwan's short reign was marked by a civil war among the Syrian Arabs as well as a war against Abdullah ibn Zubayr who continued to rule over the Hejaz, Iraq, Egypt and parts of Syria. Marwan was able to prevail in Syria at the Ba
2 CONC ttle of Marj Rahit, the result of which was a new Marwanid line of Umayyad caliphs. He was also able to recapture Egypt and Syria from Abdullah, but was not able to completely defeat him.
