1 NAME Tughril Shah, emir of Erzurum
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1225
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kayqubad_I '''Wikipedia page'''] of his nephew, Kayqubad I:
2 CONT
2 CONT When the sultan died following the battle of Alaşehir in 1211,[1] both Kayqubad and his elder brother Kaykaus struggled for the throne. Kayqubad initially garnered some support among the neighbors of the sultanate: Leo I, the king
2 CONC  of Cilician Armenia and Tughrilshah, the brothers' uncle and the independent ruler of Erzurum. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghias_ad-din '''Wikipedia page'''] of his son, Ghias ad-Din:
2 CONT
2 CONT The consort of Queen Rusudan was a younger son of 'Abdu'l Harij Muhammad Mughis ad-din Tughril Shah, the Seljuq emir of Erzurum, and his wife, a daughter of Sayf al-Din Begtimur, the ruler of Ahlat.[1] Tughril Shah had received El
2 CONC bistan in appanage upon the division of the sultanate of Rum by his father Kilij Arslan II in 1192, but he exchanged it, c. 1201, for Erzurum.[2] He appears to have been a tributary to Georgia for at least parts of his reign.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Djaparidze, Georgia and the Near Eastern Islamic world in the 12th–13th century, 1995, pp. 181–182.
2 CONT # Toumanoff, The Fifteenth-Century Bagratids and the Institution of Collegial Sovereignty in Georgia, 1949–1951, p. 181.
2 CONT # Peacock, Georgia and the Anatolian Turks in the 12th and 13th centuries, 2006, p. 130.
