1 NAME Gevher Nesibe Hatun
2 GIVN Gevher Nesibe Hatun
1 SEX F
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Çifte Madrasah in Kayseri (1206) was founded by Gevher Nesibe Hatun, the daughter of Seljuk Sultan Kilij Arslan II. The sifaiye (hospital) was completed in the reign of Kay Khusrau I in the years between 1204-1210.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT * from [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gevher_Nesibe '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gevher Nesibe''' was an early-13th-century princess of the Sultanate of Rum, the daughter of Kilij Arslan II and sister of Kaykhusraw I, and the namesake of a magnificent complex comprising a hospital, an adjoining medrese devo
2 CONC ted primarily to medical studies, and a mosque in Kayseri, Turkey. The complex (külliye in Turkish) that she endowed is considered one of the pre-eminent monuments of Seljuk architecture.
2 CONT
2 CONT The hospital was built between 1204 and 1206, and the medrese, whose construction started immediately after Gevher Nesibe's death in 1206, was finished in 1210.
2 CONT
2 CONT The complex as a whole takes its name from the princess. The medrese within is known under a variety of names: the Gevher Nesibe Medrese; the Çifte Medrese (Twin Medrese); or as the Gıyasiye Medrese, after Ghiyath al-Din Kaykhusra
2 CONC w I, who was responsible for its construction. The tomb within the medrese is said to belong to Gevher Nesibe.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Legends and the legacy=
2 CONT
2 CONT According to legend, Gevher Nesibe fell in love with a cavalry officer defending the palace of the Seljuk sultan at Konya. Her brother Kaykhusraw I opposed a marriage between his sister and an officer and sent the young man on a v
2 CONC ariety of dangerous assignments and campaigns, during the last of which he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Overcome with grief at her lover's death, Gevher Nesibe Sultan fell ill with tuberculosis.[citation needed] The remorseful Kaykhusraw visited her on her deathbed, asked her forgiveness and offered to fulfill any last wish she had.
2 CONC  Gevher Nesibe Sultan is reported to have replied: "I am very ill. There is no way for me to recover. None of these doctors can deliver me from my illness. I will eventually pass away. My wish is that you use my property to build 
2 CONC a hospital in my honor. In this hospital, sick people should be treated with no charge and at the same time, incurable illnesses researched."[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Historical record shows that Kaykhusraw carried out his sister's last wish, beginning the hospital in Kayseri in 1204. The buildings were designed and built by an architect named Üstad Ömer. The entryway inscription, a common feat
2 CONC ure of Islamic architecture that usually records the architect's name, is missing, but the information is preserved in written sources elsewhere. The institution was reportedly the first hospital in the world that treated patients
2 CONC  with mental disorders. The complex as a whole represents the earliest surviving Seljuk medrese and hospital in Anatolia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gevher Nesibe's Darüşşifa, or Şifahane, now functions as a public museum dedicated to medicine. The university hospital in Kayseri's Erciyes University was also named in memory of Gevher Nesibe.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Information: "History of Gevher Nesibe Hospital". Erciyes University.
2 CONT * İbrahim Shaikh. (full text and photos) "A visit to Gevher Nesibe Medical Museum in Kayseri in Anatolia, Turkey" (PDF). The International Society for the History of Islamic Medicine.
2 CONT * Fact sheet: "Çifte Medrese (Twin Medrese) Kayseri, Turkey". Museum of Architecture.
