0 @N6000000002188101023@ NOTE Li Chengqian, Crown Prince of Tang China
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT (Redirected from Li Chengqian)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Li Chéngqián (???) (619 - October 1, 644) was the original crown prince of Emperor Taizong of Tang China, but was replaced later by his younger brother Li Tai. His courtesy name was Gaoming (??).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was named after his birthplace, Chengqian Hall (?), and became the crown prince on October 8, 627 at the age of eight, after his father became the emperor on August 9. After September 630, his grand teacher (??) was Li Gang (??
1 CONC ); on May 6, 632, Li Gang died and was replaced by Yu Shining (???) and Li Baiyao (???). Kong Yingda (???) and Zhang Xuansu (???) were his live-in scholars (??). Taizong described his son as "understanding of the rituals" (????).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 632, with the excuse of the foot illness, he stopped attending court sessions and started to wander around the city engaging in amorous acts and imitated the practices of non-Han "barbarians". His brother Tai began to gain the 
1 CONC favor of Taizong.
1 CONT
1 CONT Li secretly housed a male musical performer called Chengxin (????), but Taizong ordered him killed later. After Chengxin's death, Li was overwhelmed with grief for months, never attending courts, and arranged the failed assassinat
1 CONC ions of Yu Shining and Zhang Xuansu, who advised him constantly to get over the pain.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 6, 643 (Zhenguan 17), after a failed rebellion, he lost his royalty status in Imperial Decree which Deposes Crown Prince Chengqian as a Commoner (??????????). He died the next year in Qian Prefecture (??) at the age of 25
1 CONC . The cause is unknown.
0 @N6000000002188101032@ NOTE Li Tai, Crown Prince of Tang China
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT (Redirected from Li Tai)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Li Tai (??; Pinyin: Li Tài) (620 - ?) was once a crown prince of the Tang Empire of China. Li was the son of Emperor Taizong of Tang China (his fourth) and Wende Empress (her second). His courtesy name was Huibao (??).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was made Prince Yidu (???) in Year Wude 3, Prince Wei (??) the next year, and made Prince Wei (??) in Zhenguan 2, and Prince Bu (??) in Zhenguan 21. He was also named Prince of Donglai District (????) and Prince Shunyang (???).
1 CONT
1 CONT After his elder brother Crown Prince Li Chengqian fell out of favour with Taizong, Tai was given the position of Governor of Xiang Commandry (???? Xiangzhou Dudu). However, Tai refused the position and stayed in the capital. Taizo
1 CONC ng commanded the establishment of a scholar academy in the prince's residence and increased his salary over that of his elder brother. Moreover, Taizong invited Tai to move into Dewu Hall (???) of the imperial palace. Most officia
1 CONC ls were against these acts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Li Tai was made the new crown prince after his brother tried to rebel. However, after being made the crown prince, Li Tai tried to hurt Li Chengqian numerous times and exhibited an overwhelming vicious ambition. So Taizong replace
1 CONC d Li Tai with the more popular "virtuous" Li Zhi as the next crown prince. Li Zhi became Emperor Gaozong of Tang China.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Buddhist statues in the Main Wall of Bingyang South Cave (??????) of the Longmen Caves was dedicated by Li Tai to his deceased mother, Gongsun-shi.
0 @N6000000002188099899@ NOTE Emperor Gaozong of Tang
